{"ID": "soap_00057", "dialogue": "How are you, sir? One less than sixty. So, what happened? I was visiting my son in Cheyenne, Wyoming, then I on October tenth when I was taking a shower, I felt a pop in my head. I don't know what happened after that. Guest_family: We thought he was having a long shower that day. Then about one and a half hours later my son suspected something is not right and we went up to check. There we found him unconscious on the shower floor. Our son, he then drove us back to Iowa and since then he is complaining of these headaches and that he's not feeling well. He gets unconscious or falls asleep I don't know but it is happening a lot of the time. We got him to local hospital today in the morning. It is three days after that shower incident. Yeah, his report says he got a hemorrhage, I mean, blood clot in his brain. Guest_family: Yeah, after that report they transferred us to this hospital.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient felt a \"pop\" in his head while taking a shower on October 10th.\n- Unconscious for an unknown duration, found on the shower floor by his son about one and a half hours later.\n- Complaining of headaches since the incident.\n- Reports feeling unwell and frequently becoming unconscious or falling asleep.\n- Family history includes a recent visit to a local hospital where he was diagnosed with a hemorrhage (blood clot) in the brain.\n- Emotional concern from family regarding the patient's condition.\n\nObjective:\n- Patient's report from the local hospital indicates a hemorrhage (blood clot) in the brain.\n \nAssessment:\n- Diagnosis of hemorrhage (blood clot) in the brain based on hospital report.\n\nPlan:\n- Transferred to another hospital for further evaluation and treatment. \n- No specific medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00026", "dialogue": "How are you feeling today? I am feeling very tired. According to your chart, you have seen with us for three separate self harm attempts. Have you had any other self harm events that you have not come to see use for? No. I have been seeing Doctor Lin. I also see that this is your third time in the last two months that you have attempted to harm yourself. Is there anything that is currently going on in your life that could be triggering these resent episode? Last month I lost my job. And then this month my boyfriend left me. I am so sorry. That is a lot to deal with. Yeah, it has been hard. Have you been diagnosed with any mental health conditions? Doctor Lin just said that I was depressed. Have you ever been put on any medication for the for your depression? I have taken Lexapro before. How long ago did you take the Lexapro? I can't remember. Okay.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Feeling very tired, depressed.\n- Severity: Not explicitly stated, but the context implies significant distress.\n- Duration: Recent episodes of self-harm; third attempt in the last two months.\n- Associated Symptoms: Emotional distress related to job loss and relationship ending.\n- Relevant Medical History: Previous diagnosis of depression by Doctor Lin; history of self-harm (three attempts).\n- Family History: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other Concerns: Recently lost job and boyfriend, leading to emotional difficulties.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Diagnosis: Depression (previously diagnosed by Doctor Lin).\n- Clinical Impression: Patient is experiencing significant emotional distress due to life events (job loss, relationship issues) and has a history of self-harm.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific plan mentioned in the conversation."}
{"ID": "soap_00005", "dialogue": "Hi there! What brings you in today? Guest_family: I think my baby got into the ant bait. I am not sure if he consumed any of it but he was under the counter and it was in his hands. What kind ant bait did he get into? Guest_family: It was the one with Borax in it. Do you have a picture of it? Guest_family: Yes. It is in my phone.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Concern that the baby may have gotten into ant bait containing Borax.\n- Uncertainty about whether the baby consumed any of it.\n- The baby was found under the counter with the ant bait in his hands.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnosis provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00013", "dialogue": "Do you know when was the last time you got a tetanus shot? No I don't remember. Okay.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient does not remember the last time they received a tetanus shot.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00061", "dialogue": "So, looks like you have stones in your C B D. I am not hundred percent sure but most likely its stones. C B D? Yeah, short for common bile duct. It's a small tube like structure which connects your liver duct and gallbladder. Oh! Yeah, and because of that you have inflammation in your gallbladder. Oh, I see.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient has stones in the common bile duct (CBD).\n- Patient has inflammation in the gallbladder.\n- Patient expresses understanding of the condition when explained.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely presence of stones in the common bile duct.\n- Inflammation of the gallbladder.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, or follow-up mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00024", "dialogue": "Hey, what's going on with you today? Oh, I have a chest pain. You have fever too? Yes, I did.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Chief complaint: chest pain\n- Associated symptom: fever (reported as having fever)\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment information provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan information provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00022", "dialogue": "Good morning. How are you feeling today? I feel good, considering. I slept well last night. Are you expensing any pain or discomfort? No. I feel fine.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient feels good, considering.\n- Slept well last night.\n- No pain or discomfort reported.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00038", "dialogue": "Uh, what brings you in here today? Yeah, so my daughter, she's had this hoarse voice for the past like two or three days. It doesn't seem to be getting better. In fact, it's getting worse and she just doesn't sound like herself anymore and she's not speaking as much. OK, and when did this start? OK, so she had a cough like four days ago, and she's still coughing a little bit, but not as much as she was before, but it's more her voice that I'm concerned about. It sounds very hoarse and harsh. OK, and so how many days has she had the hoarse voice for? I'd say about two or three days now. Two or three days, OK. And when she was coughing, you mentioned like previously was she coughing up any sputum or was it more of a dry cough? It was more of a dry cough And the cough has pretty much resolved by now? Yeah, she still gets them once in a while, but yeah, it's pretty much gone. OK, and when she was coughing, did she at all bring up any kind of blood or any green or yellowish material? No No, OK. Did she have any fevers at all, recently? Uh, no, no fevers. OK. And does she have any kind of, uh, along with a hoarse voice, does she have any pain with talking? Yes, I think so. OK, and does she have any pain with swallowing any foods or liquids? She's eating and drinking ok, but I suspect there is some pain. OK, So how long has she, so she has a sore throat, how long has she had that for? The sore throat? Uhm I would say for the past two or three days as well. OK, and was there anything that, so she had kind of like a cough previously, but anything else, anyone sick around her, or anything you think might have precipitated some of her symptoms? No, not that I know of. I don't think she's had any contacts with sick people recently. OK. Are there any, have you tried any medications over the counter, that has helped her? Uhm, not over the counter medications, but like I tried to get her to like gargle with like salt water and all that kind of stuff, but that didn't seem to help. OK. And have her symptoms been just constant or do they come and go? They've been pretty constant. OK, and you think her symptoms are getting worse? Uh, no, they've been pretty consistent, but maybe the harsh voice is getting worse. OK. And other than the symptoms that you've mentioned, has she had any kind of shortness of breath, wheezing, chest pain or anything like that? No, nothing like that. OK, and no fevers, right? No OK. Has she traveled at all recently outside of the country or outside of the province? No, no, nothing like that. OK, and any headaches at all? Uhm, no. No, any nausea or vomiting? Uh, no, nothing like that. OK, any runny nose or congestion? No. No, OK. And have you had a chance to at all, look into her mouth and throat? And if you have, have you noticed any kind of redness, swelling? I haven't looked in there too much, like, just an inspection the other day, it looked a little bit red, but I didn't see any discharge. OK, and have you noticed any kind of swelling around her neck, ears, like, around her lymph nodes? Uhm, I haven't actually taken a look there. OK, we can do that. Other than those symptoms, has she been experiencing any kind of like fatigue or have you notice her being just very tired? Um, she has seemed a little bit more tired than usual. OK, and right now is she going to school or is right now all like virtual? Is she at home? Uhm, she's at home right now. OK, and does she have any symptoms of, any chest pain at all? No chest pain. OK, any changes in her bowel movements, any Constipation, diarrhea? No. OK, any urinary problems? No. OK, has she been able to sleep well or does she wake up a lot more at night? Uhm, she's been able to sleep well, yeah OK, how about her appetite? Its been pretty normal. She's not eating as much as she used to, but she's still eating and drinking ok. OK, and have you noticed any kind of weight changes at all recently has she lost any weight? No OK, and, has she had any loss of taste, of smell or sorry, loss of sense of taste or smell at all. Uh, no. Any muscle aches or anything like that? Uh, no, nothing like that. OK, any exposure to fumes, dust, animals or Pets at Home? Uh, no, nothing like that. OK, has this happened at all in the past? Uhm, no. No, OK. Does she have any medical conditions at all that she's been diagnosed with in the past? Uhm, no, nothing like that. Any like asthma or any other conditions? No. OK, any allergies that you know of? I know she has seasonal allergies, but that's about it. OK, and does she take any medications at all on a daily basis? Uh, no, she just takes multi vitamins. OK, and currently or previously has she had any surgeries or hospitalizations? No. No, OK. Are there any family history of any lung or heart conditions? Uh, no. Any cancers? There's some colon cancer on her dad side of the family. OK, and is there any, so do you remember who the person is and also like when they got diagnosed with the colon cancer? So it was her uncle. He was diagnosed when he was forty five. OK, other than that, were there any developmental challenges at all while she was growing up? Did she, was she meeting her milestones? Any concerns at all? OK, and uh, in terms of her pregnancy, were there any, or your pregnancy with her, were there any complications at all either during the pregnancy or after she was born? And right now, where does she live, and who does she live with? she's at home with myself, my husband and she has one younger sibling. OK and, is everyone at home well, does anyone have any of these symptoms? No, OK. Right, so, and in terms of the home environment, does anyone smoke in the house? No. No OK, alright so I think that's all the questions I had right now. Do you have any questions or concerns? No, no questions, but uhm, actually, what do you think she has and how can we treat her? Yeah, so it seems like that the hoarseness of voice and the sore throat and her symptoms mostly align with what's called laryngitis, and this is usually caused by like just a viral respiratory infection. The hoarseness of voice will usually last more than a week and up to like ten days and it should resolve on its own. But we'll definitely take a better look and do a proper physical examination, see if I see any kind of swelling, redness, or any like white streaks or exudates around her pharynx as well. So after we take a look, we'll see what we can do. It doesn't seem like, it can also be a bacterial infection, in which case we would need to give her antibiotics, but usually it's a viral infection that should go away on its own. So we would just kind of wait and see and potentially just give her voice some rest during this period, and just go on from there. Do you have any other questions or concerns? Uh no, that sounds great, thank you. You're welcome and yeah, we'll, just do a full examination and then go from there. OK, sounds great thanks. You're welcome. Take care. You too.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 3 yo F presents with hoarse voice x2-3 days, worsening\n- Cough x4 days, mostly resolved, dry, no sputum production, hemoptysis or colored sputum\n- Sore throat x2-3 days\n- No known sick contacts\n- Tried salt water gargles without improvement\n- Symptoms constant, hoarseness worsening\n- Denies SOB, wheezing, chest pain, fever, headache, N/V, rhinorrhea, congestion, fatigue\n- Mild decrease in appetite but still eating and drinking normally\n- No changes in BMs or urinary symptoms\n- Sleeping well\n- No recent travel, exposure to fumes, dust, animals\n- No previous similar episodes\n\nPMHx:\n- Seasonal allergies\n- No other medical conditions or surgeries\n- No medications except multivitamins\n\nFHx:\n- Paternal uncle dx with colon cancer at age 45\n- No family history of lung or heart conditions\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives at home with parents and younger sibling\n- No smokers in the household\n- Meeting developmental milestones\n\nObjective:\n- Inspection of throat: mild erythema, no discharge\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely viral laryngitis\n\nPlan:\n- Will perform full physical exam including inspection of pharynx for erythema, edema, exudates\n- Likely viral and should resolve spontaneously within 1-2 weeks\n- Advise voice rest\n- If suggestive of bacterial infection, will treat with antibiotics\n- Follow up if no improvement or worsening symptoms"}
{"ID": "soap_00025", "dialogue": "Anyone in the family had any medical issues? Oh yes. Can you please elaborate? So, my mother's side family had heart issues. My dad's sister had sugar problem.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient's mother's side family has a history of heart issues.\n- Patient's father's sister had a history of diabetes (sugar problem).\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment information provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan information provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00052", "dialogue": "What brings you in? Uh I just came in today I think I broke my leg or something, it's something that was, I heard a snap, I was in um just a dance class um and we have like these long uh dresses, it it was a traditional dance class and my foot uh slipped right onto, it got caught in my dress and it just kind of slipped and I I felt right on my, my leg kind of twisted and I fell down and I just stayed down. I knew something was not right and something was broken. When did this happen? Just uh I would say like an hour ago Hour ago, OK. Are you able to bear any weight on it at all? No, not really. I I had to come in the ambulance. OK, OK, and um if you were to localize this pain, where exactly is it? It's pretty much my whole lower leg is painful, but I think where it looks like it broke is like the middle part of my lower leg. The middle part, OK middle part of your lower leg, so kind of where your shins are? Mhmm. OK, so in the shin area, OK. Um have you tried anything for it? I know it's only been an hour. Uh no nothing. I just like I just want to make sure I keep it as still as possible um I didn't wanna even try anything. OK yeah, that makes sense, if you were to describe this pain, would you say it's um sharp, dull, achy throbbing? It's like sharp and throbbing. It's like probably one of the worst pains that I've felt in my life. OK, OK. And 1 to 10, 10 being the worst pain ever, how bad is it? At 10 for sure. Ten, OK, have you, so you said you've never experienced this before, um do you feel like it's getting worse, the pain, or has it been pretty much the same since it started, or is it getting better? Uh I would say it's the same, it's like I'm in as much pain as I was earlier. OK. OK and um so yeah, OK, um do you know how you felt like on the floor? Like did you uh go like leg first or was it, did your um, kind of like the mechanism of your fall, did you break the fall at all with your arms? Uh no, I didn't break, it kind of, so I was kind of uh, I feel like when I was slipping it was at first my leg was hyper extended but it kind of just felt like it snapped and it went straight down, so there was some kind of twisting as well as some hyperextension. Um but it just kind of slipping on my dress and then just straight down. OK, OK. Um otherwise, do you have any pain elsewhere in your body? No. How about any numbness or tingling in the area? Um I do feel some numbness, tingling just below where it happened, um but it like comes and goes. OK um and then how about how about any um like feelings of like losing sensation or feeling cold in that area? Um no, I I can feel everything in that area. OK. But it's hard to tell like it's just very painful right now. OK, yeah, that's understandable. Um how about headaches? No, no headaches. Uh any fainting or dizziness. No. Um confusion or memory loss? No. Um weight loss recently. No. Fevers or chills? No. Uh changes to your hair, nails, or skin, like rashes? No, nothing that I've noticed. No, any changes to your vision, hearing, smell, or taste? No. Um how about coughing, shortness of breath, or wheezing? No, no, nothing like that. No, OK, um how about chest pain or palpitations? No. No, any changes to your bowel movements? Uh no, not that I've noticed. Uh nausea or vomiting? Nope. Abdominal pain? No. Changes to urinary habits? No, nothing like that. No, OK, um and are you otherwise healthy? Yeah, I've been pretty healthy, I um don't take any medications regularly, I don't have any medical conditions. OK, that's good. Any hospitalizations or surgeries in the past? No, um just just for like my pregnancies, I had two pregnancies, both of them delivered vaginally, but no issues with that, just after that no hospitalizations or surgeries. OK, OK. Um and then um are you up to date with all your immunizations? Yep. OK, that's good. Um do you have any allergies? No, not that I know of. OK, um and then is there any family history of like musculoskeletal issues? Um so my mother has something called osteoporosis. OK, yep. Um but that's that's all that I can think of. How old was she when she was diagnosed with that? Um probably in her fifties. In her fifties, OK. Late fifties I would say. Um have you been, have you ever like been tested for that? No, I've never, I've never had testing. OK, all right. Um and then any family history of lung or heart conditions? No. Uh how about autoimmune conditions? Uh no, not that I know of. Any cancers? Uh, I think one of my aunts has uh ovarian cancer. OK, OK, um how old was she when she was diagnosed with that? Um like pretty uh recently, so probably like in her sixties. OK, OK, um and then have you been in touch with anyone sick recently? No. No, OK, have you done any travel recently? No. OK. Um those are all the questions I had for you. Did you have any questions of your own or anything you wanted to add before we move to the physical exam component? No, I just want to get this fixed as soon as possible. OK, that sounds good, yeah we'll, we'll try our best. Thank you. Um OK, so now looking at your legs like side by side, do you notice any uh redness or swelling in your right leg compared to your left? Yeah, it's all, it's all swollen and red and just looks so much different than my other leg. OK, yeah, um do you notice any obvious like breaks in the skin? No, there's no breaks in the skin but like when I fell like I, I can definitely see like the bend, there is like a bend in the bone, so I definitely do think it's broken. OK, and do you notice any like bony pieces that are pushing on the skin? No. No, OK. Uh any bruises in the area? Yeah, the whole area seems bruised up and angry. OK. Um does uh, besides the bruising and the misalignment, do you notice any other obvious deformities, any muscle atrophy in the area? No. No, OK, and then uh placing your hand on one leg compared to the other, does it seem warmer? Yeah, I feel like it's it's warmer. OK um and then I'm gonna try to get you to do some movements, um can you move your right leg at all, like can you flex and extend your knees? No, not not at all like I I'm I'm too scared to try. OK, and how about weight bearing, are you able to stand up at all on that leg? No, I just I just got time to call the ambulance, I knew I knew it was just not possible. OK, alright, um I guess those were all the questions that I had for you. Um I think you may have a tibial fracture. So we'll start with some X Rays um to be sure and to try to take a look at like where the fracture is and how we can approach that. Um because everything is swollen and angry down there, we'll probably try and um apply a splint um until the swelling goes down. And then um we can uh refer you to an orthopaedic surgeon for a surgic, for the surgery itself. So that could um involve, like uh you know them doing like and intramedullary nail, plates and screws, and um like some sort of fixation. Um but after the surgery, your fracture will probably take about four to six months to heal completely, uh so in that period um we can talk about like pain management and physiotherapy as well, just to keep the muscles in that area strong and to increase your range of motion. Does that sound OK? That sounds good. Alright, thank you so much. OK, yeah, no worries.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presents with right leg pain and deformity after a fall during a traditional dance class (long dress got caught on foot causing slip and fall with leg twisting)\n- Fall occurred 1 hour ago\n- Unable to bear weight \n- Pain localized to middle part of lower leg/shin area\n- Sharp, throbbing pain rated 10/10 in severity \n- Pain has remained constant since onset\n- Some intermittent numbness and tingling below the site of injury\n- Denies loss of sensation or coldness in the affected area\n- Denies headache, dizziness, confusion, memory loss, weight loss, fever, chills, skin/hair/nail changes, vision/hearing/smell/taste changes, cough, shortness of breath, wheezing, chest pain, palpitations, bowel/bladder changes, nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain\n\nPMHx:\n- No chronic medical conditions\n- No medications\n- No allergies\n- Surgical history significant for 2 uncomplicated vaginal deliveries\n- Mother with history of osteoporosis diagnosed in her 50s\n- Maternal aunt with history of ovarian cancer diagnosed in her 60s\n- Denies personal history of musculoskeletal, pulmonary, cardiac, autoimmune conditions, or cancer\n- Up to date with immunizations\n- Denies recent sick contacts or travel\n\nObjective:\n- Obvious deformity of right lower leg with visible bending of the bone\n- Swelling, redness, and bruising of right lower leg\n- No open wounds or bony protrusion through skin\n- Increased warmth of right lower leg compared to left\n- Unable to actively flex or extend right knee due to pain\n- Unable to bear weight on right leg\n\nAssessment:\n- Suspected right tibial fracture\n\nPlan:\n- X-rays of right tibia/fibula to assess for fracture location and characteristics\n- Splint application to affected leg until swelling subsides\n- Referral to orthopedic surgery for definitive management (likely intramedullary nailing vs open reduction internal fixation with plates/screws)\n- Anticipate 4-6 month healing time\n- Pain management\n- Physical therapy for strength and range of motion after surgical fixation"}
{"ID": "soap_00085", "dialogue": "Do you have any known allergies to medication? Nope. None that I know of.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No known allergies to medications.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00067", "dialogue": "Do you smoke? Yes. How much do you smoke? Honestly, I smoke every day.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient smokes every day.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00079", "dialogue": "So, do you drink or smoke, sir? I'd say I have the occasional drink, but I don't smoke. What do you do for a living? I'm a nursing aid in a nursing home right now. Do you work the normal day shift? No, I work the second shift, so I work some late nights and early mornings. Are you back at Wichita State this semester? No, I decided to take some time off. Is everything okay? Yeah, I'm just really tired, so I decided to step away for a little. That's understandable. Are you dating right now? Yes, I've been dating my boyfriend for a while now. Is there any significant stress in your relationship? No, everything is going really well.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Tiredness\n- Severity: Not explicitly mentioned\n- Duration: Ongoing, as the patient decided to take some time off due to tiredness\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: None provided\n- Family history: None provided\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other concerns: The patient is currently taking time off from school and works second shift as a nursing aid, which may contribute to tiredness. The relationship with the boyfriend is positive, with no significant stress reported.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00044", "dialogue": "Tell me about your family medical history. My family is pretty healthy. I don't know of any health problems.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Family medical history: Patient reports that their family is pretty healthy and they do not know of any health problems.\n\nObjective:\n- [No measurable or observable data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00042", "dialogue": "How old are you, sir? I'm sixty five years old. Are you right handed or left handed? I'm right handed. When was your last stroke? Well, it was on January seventeenth. I had these episodes where I was like always so confused and looks like I lost everything. All my memory and everything. And then they did a scan. It was verified that I was having a stroke on the same day. Did they put you on some medications? They gave me an anticonvulsant and aspirin. Are you taking them regularly? There have a few times like one or two days that I forgot to take my, uh, dyphenhydramine, but even when I'm taking it regularly, I'm still having all those symptoms or forgetfulness, confusion, and everything. Do you feel anything else along with these episodes like a urine urgency, tendency to bite your tongue, headaches, any visual change, or your heart is beating very fast? Any kind of weakness or numbness or shortness of breath? No nothing else, just the confusion and forgetfulness. How frequent are these episodes? Well, they occur like two or three times in a week. Each one of the episodes lasts for like one or two minutes.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Confusion, forgetfulness\n- Severity: Not explicitly stated, but implied to be significant due to impact on memory.\n- Duration: Episodes occur 2-3 times a week; each episode lasts for 1-2 minutes.\n- Associated symptoms: None reported (urinary urgency, tongue biting, headaches, visual changes, heart palpitations, weakness, numbness, or shortness of breath not present).\n- Relevant medical history: Previous stroke on January 17th; diagnosed with stroke on the same day as symptoms.\n- Medications: Anticonvulsant and aspirin prescribed; occasional missed doses of diphenhydramine.\n- Concerns: Ongoing symptoms of confusion and forgetfulness even when medications are taken regularly.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided in the conversation)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnosis provided in the conversation)\n\nPlan:\n- (No specific plan mentioned in the conversation)"}
{"ID": "soap_00004", "dialogue": "It sounds like that you're experiencing some chest pain. Yeah, so this chest pain has just been coming on for the last couple of weeks now. OK, uhm. So 2 weeks, and can you tell me kind of where you're feeling that pain? Um yeah, I'm I'm just feeling this chest discomfort, sort of over on the left side. The left side, OK. Does does it stay just in one spot or does it kind of spread anywhere else? No, I don't really feel it anywhere else. Yeah, I just feel it in that one spot on the left side. OK, does it feel dull and achy, or does it feel more sharp? It feels pretty sharp. OK, and is it constant like on all the time, or do you feel like it kind of comes and goes? It comes and goes. I would say I it might last for um 20-30 minutes at a time and then it goes away. OK, and over the last two weeks since you said it started, has it gotten any worse? Uh, no. It's been about the same. About the same, OK, and has it gotten more frequent, these like 20 to 30 minute episodes? No, it's happened about two or three times during the 2 weeks. And if you were to rate it on a scale of zero to 10, zero being no pain and 10 being like the worst pain you've ever felt, where would it fall? I would say a 4 out of 10. OK. Uh, can you think of anything that that you've done differently recently that could have brought it on or is there any kind of common factor between the times that you had the pain? Um, I don't know I've separated from my husband 2 two years ago which has been really stressful but other than that I mean I can't really think of any changes to my health or anything like that recently. OK, but do you feel like you have been more stressed out in general? Uh, yeah the last couple of years have been a lot more stressful because of those issues in my personal life. OK, I'm sorry to hear that, but I'm glad that you're coming in and we can hopefully get to the bottom of this. Not that I think that's necessarily the source of your pain, and we definitely want to look into everything, but it is really helpful to know that. So when you've had these episodes, have you taken anything to help with the pain or done anything that's that you felt it made it go away? Uhm, I've tried some Advil, some Tylenol and actually some Tums as well, and it hasn't really helped. OK, do you think it's worse when you're moving around, if you're if you're kind of doing more exercise, or is it kind of just the same no matter what you're doing when you get the episodes? Oh, it actually I I feel like it happens most of the time when I'm either at work or or driving. OK. OK, and before this two week period, have you ever had anything like this before? No, I haven't had anything like this happen before. Do you have any chronic conditions that you see your family doctor for? No, previously healthy. And just jumping back to these pain episodes when you have them, do you experience anything else? Do you get any nausea or vomiting? No. OK, do you get any, do you have any difficulty breathing during these times? No, I haven't. OK, and have you noticed any fevers? Uh, no, no fevers. OK, have you been in contact with anyone that's been sick recently? Uhm, no, I haven't. And have you traveled anywhere outside of Canada? No, I've been home. OK so you have, you said you have been previously healthy, nothing that you've been seeing your family doctor for on a regular basis. Do you take any medications? Uhm, I do not, no medications, just the Tylenol and Advil I tried for the pain. OK, do you have any allergies? No allergies. And is there any history of heart problems in your family? Yeah, my father had a heart attack when he was 60 just last year and it's actually one of the reasons what concerns me about the chest pain that I thought I was having. For sure, it always feels very concerning when when you have these these symptoms and that and you know people in your life that have had issues like similar issues in the past. I don't know that this is related to that. I I personally don't think so at this point, but we're definitely going to do everything to look into it and and rule out something that would be scary like that. I'm going to ask you few more questions about your lifestyle in general. Do you smoke cigarettes? Yeah, I smoke about half a pack to a pack per day. I've been trying to quit and I was smoking more one pack per day, but so it is down a little bit, but, It's still something I'm trying to work on. Congratulations, that's a really tough thing to do so, good for you. That's that's really exciting. Step in the right direction, right? Do you drink alcohol? Um, I do, but, uh, I'll have maybe one or two glasses of wine on the weekend. Do you use any other substances? Uh no. I know that you said you don't see your family doctor for anything regularly, but I just want to check a couple of specific things like have you ever been told you have high blood pressure? No, I've never been told I've I've had high blood pressure. And have you ever been told you have like high cholesterol? I haven't, but I've actually never been tested either. OK, and do you exercise regularly? Yeah, actually, I'll either do some running or cycling, probably three or four days per week and I haven't had any chest pain or or problems exercising. OK, that's good to hear. And have you ever had problems with heartburn? Uh. I don't think so, no. OK. OK, so just to kind of summarize what we've talked about, over the last two weeks, you started having these 20 to 30 minute episodes of chest pain that's on the left side, it feels more sharp, is that correct? Yeah. And it's kind of constant when it comes on, there's nothing really that makes it better or worse, it just kind of goes away on its own after half an hour or so, it has kind of stayed the same over over the last two weeks, hasn't really gotten worse or better, it hasn't gone away either? Right, yeah exactly. You never had anything like that before and you have been, it sounds like experiencing some stress in your life, it definitely can play into that as well. OK, is there anything else that you wanted to to tell me about that I didn't ask you today? Uh, no, I think that's um, yeah, I think that's all that I wanted to talk about today. OK, so what I'm going to do is bring all this information to your family doctor and we can come back together and talk to you about the next steps. Awesome, thank you. Thanks for coming in.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presenting with chest pain for the last 2 weeks\n- Location: left-sided chest discomfort\n- Quality: sharp pain\n- Severity: 4/10\n- Timing: intermittent, lasting 20-30 minutes per episode\n- Context: occurs most often while at work or driving\n- Alleviating/Aggravating factors: no clear aggravating or alleviating factors identified; pain is not exacerbated by physical activity\n- Associated symptoms: denies SOB, N/V, or fevers\n- Self-treatment: tried Advil, Tylenol, and Tums without relief\n\nPMHx:\n- Previously healthy\n- Denies chronic conditions or regular follow-up with PCP\n- Medications: none; has only tried Advil and Tylenol for current chest pain\n- Allergies: NKDA\n- FHx: father had MI at age 60 last year\n\nSocHx:\n- Separated from husband 2 years ago, causing significant stress\n- Tobacco: current smoker, 0.5-1 PPD; has reduced from 1 PPD and is actively trying to quit\n- Alcohol: 1-2 glasses of wine on weekends\n- Denies other substance use\n- Exercise: runs or cycles 3-4 days/week without chest pain or issues\n\nROS:\n- Denies prior history of HTN or hyperlipidemia, but has never been tested for the latter\n- Denies history of GERD\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals signs not provided\n- Physical examination not performed\n\nAssessment:\n- Differential diagnosis not provided\n\nPlan:\n- Investigations: none mentioned\n- Treatment: none mentioned\n- Other: will discuss case with PCP and follow up with patient regarding next steps"}
{"ID": "soap_00069", "dialogue": "Any health issues on your mom or dad's side? No, we're a pretty healthy family.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No health issues reported on mother's or father's side.\n- The patient reports that their family is a \"pretty healthy family.\"\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00077", "dialogue": "Okay, I want you to follow up with our hand therapist Doctor X at her A B C D office in one to two weeks. Okay, I will make an appointment today.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is following up with hand therapy.\n- No specific symptoms, severity, or duration mentioned.\n- The name of the hand therapist and office location provided (Doctor X at A B C D office).\n- The patient expresses intent to make an appointment.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnosis provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Follow up with hand therapist Doctor X at A B C D office in one to two weeks.\n- Patient will make an appointment today."}
{"ID": "soap_00030", "dialogue": "So, how are feeling? You came here last time with C V A right? Yeah, I am recovering well. You asked me to follow-up for high sugar. Oh yeah, you have diabetes mellitus. Did you happen to get a blood test done before this visit? Yes, I did here is the report.", "note": "Subjective:\n- The patient is recovering well from a cerebrovascular accident (CVA).\n- The patient has diabetes mellitus.\n- The patient followed up for high sugar.\n- The patient brought a blood test report to the visit.\n\nObjective:\n- Blood test report presented by the patient.\n\nAssessment:\n- Diabetes mellitus.\n- Follow-up after CVA.\n\nPlan:\n- Review the blood test report.\n- Continue monitoring of diabetes management."}
{"ID": "soap_00002", "dialogue": "Do you have any known allergies to medications? No.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No known allergies to medications.\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00047", "dialogue": "Welcome in. Thank you. What do you do for work? I work in Human Resources for the State of Maryland. Do you live alone or with others? I live alone. Do you smoke cigarettes? I used to smoke like a chimney. I quit smoking a while back. I had been smoking for about sixteen years. That's great! When did you quit? I quit in nineteen eighty four. That is also the year I quit drinking. What was the reason behind quitting drinking? I had a drinking problem. I am in A A. I have been in A A since I quit. That's great. Keep up the hard work. Thank you. Do you have any history of recreational or I V drug use? Yes. I used to take pills, but I never got into needle drugs. Thank God. I went through treatment, all those years back. I started going to A A and got my life together. Do you have any other risk factors for H I V? Like multiple sex partners? No. I had my last H I V test over two years ago. I have been with the same guy for over two years. Okay.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient works in Human Resources for the State of Maryland.\n- Lives alone.\n- Former smoker; quit smoking in 1984 after smoking for about sixteen years.\n- Quit drinking in 1984 due to a drinking problem; has been in Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) since then.\n- Used to take pills but never used needle drugs; went through treatment in the past.\n- Last HIV test was over two years ago; has been with the same partner for over two years.\n- No mention of current symptoms, concerns, or emotional state.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided in the conversation.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00094", "dialogue": "What brings you in? Hi. Uh, I've just had this cough for the past week and I was worried I might have Covid so I just wanted to um I just wanted to be checked. Sure, so you mentioned you, it started a week ago. Yeah. Has it been persistent or does it come and go? Um it's just been there. Been there. OK. Yeah, I just get this itchy sort of uh like scratchy sensation in my throat. And then I just cough and um that makes it better. OK. OK, uhm what, how would you describe your cough like is it barky? Is it harsh? Is it muffled? Uhm no, I don't I don't think it's barky or harsh. OK, and does anything make it better or worse like you know, like the cold air, laying down, at particular time of day? Not really, I haven't uh, nothing's really helped. Um I also tried a cough syrup, um it didn't, it helped for a couple hours, but then it was back again. The cough was back again. OK, and have you ever experienced anything like this in the past? Uh well, I I said yeah when I get a cold. Mhmm, OK. Once a year maybe. Once a year? OK. Um do you experience any shortness of breath with this cough? No. No, OK. Is it a dry cough or do you bring up any phlegm? No, it's a dry cough. OK, uh any blood that you've seen? No, no. No, OK. And do you feel wheezy? No. No, chest pain? No. No, um feeling like your heart's racing? No. Any swelling in your limbs? Uh no. No, any fainting spells? Uhm, no. No, OK. Do you ever feel like your nose is stuffy or runny? No. No, any changes in your vision or hearing? Uh no. A sore throat? No. Um loss of smell or taste? No. Any weight loss? Uh no. Fevers? I checked no, no, I don't have a fever. No, any rashes? No. Issues uh, any changes in your bowel movements? Um uh no, I've just been constipated from time to time, but that's nothing, that's not new. OK, constipation. And I'm sorry when did your constipation start? Oh it's a problem for years. I don't eat enough fibre. Oh, OK. Any changes in your urine? No. OK, any nausea or vomiting? Uh, no. No, OK. Any joint pain? No. No, numbness or tingling? No. Headaches? No. No, OK. Are you otherwise healthy? Do you have any longstanding medical conditions? I have diabetes. OK, how long have you had diabetes? It's about, it's been uh seven years now. OK, any hospitalizations or surgeries in the past? No. OK and um how about medications? Are you on any? Uh yeah, I've been taking uh metformin for the past uh, for the past five years, when, five or six years. OK. Um and uh my family Doc started me on uh ramipril about two weeks ago, he told me that uh now that I'm 50, I need to I need to be on this pill as well, so now I have to take 2 pills instead of one. OK. Yeah because I think it's it's good for my heart or something. Yeah, for sure. So you started that two weeks ago, OK. Are you up to date with all your immunizations? Uh yeah, I think so. OK, any allergies? Uhm penicillin, I just get uh I get a rash. I got a rash when I was a child, I haven't had it since then. OK, OK. Any family history of lung or heart conditions? Uh no, nothing like that. No, OK. Do you smoke? Uh, no. No, how about, uh, marijuana or other recreational drugs? No. No, OK, um alcohol? Uh yeah, I drink on the weekends. OK, what do you drink and how much do you drink? I'll have maybe two or three uh bottles of beer. OK sure, and um have you been in contact with any sick people recently? No, no, I've just been staying at home.I'm just scared of covid, but I'm I'm still worried I might have somehow gotten it. Yeah, for sure. Uhm, any travel recently? No. No, OK. And where do you live right now? I live in a house with my wife and my two kids. OK, uh are they healthy? Yeah, they're all healthy. OK. Any old carpet, dust, mold, or asbestos in your home? Um not that I'm aware of. No, OK. OK, well it seems like um those are all the questions that I had for you today. Did you have any questions of your own? Uh no, I just want to get tested for Covid. Yeah, so we will definitely do a Covid swab on you because you have a cough, um and those results should come back to you in about 24 to 48 hours, and so you'll know more then. But because your symptoms do overlap with that of Covid, we still recommend isolating for 14 days. But the other thing I want to mention is that you don't have any other symptoms of infection besides your cough, and it seems like the cough started a week ago and you started this new medication ramipril two weeks ago, and so ramipril is a type of ACE inhibitor and one of its side effects is that in some people it can cause coughing. So, I am going to review your medications and if your family Doc also agrees, we may try uh another um blood pressure medication for you instead of ramipril. Sure. Um so we'll try and change that medication and see if your coughing stops. How does that sound? Sounds great. OK, great, thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Cough for the past week\n- Persistent, dry cough with itchy, scratchy throat sensation\n- Cough syrup provided temporary relief\n- No SOB, chest pain, wheezing, blood in sputum, or fever\n- No nasal congestion, sore throat, loss of smell/taste, weight loss, rashes, or changes in bowel movements (constipation noted but longstanding)\n- No changes in vision, hearing, urine, or bowel movements\n- No nausea, vomiting, joint pain, numbness, tingling, or headaches\n- No recent contact with sick individuals or travel\n\nPast Medical History:\n- PMHx: Diabetes (7 years)\n- Medications: Metformin (5-6 years), Ramipril (2 weeks)\n- Allergies: Penicillin (rash as a child)\n- Immunizations: Up to date\n- SocHx: No smoking, marijuana, or recreational drugs; alcohol (2-3 beers on weekends)\n- FHx: No lung or heart conditions\n- Lives with wife and 2 children, no known environmental hazards at home\n\nObjective:\n- No vitals or physical examination findings provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Possible COVID-19 (cough, recent onset)\n- Possible Ramipril-induced cough (started 2 weeks ago)\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 swab (results in 24-48 hours)\n- Recommend isolation for 14 days\n- Review and possibly change blood pressure medication (Ramipril) if family doctor agrees"}
{"ID": "soap_00001", "dialogue": "What brings you in here today? Hi, I'm um, I'm just, so I am here because I have this cough that I've had for the past um, week and a half, and I'm just uh, I usually don't come in with symptoms this minor, but because it's, because we're in the middle of a pandemic, I thought I should come in and get checked out. Okay, yeah, so it's been going on for like more than a week. Um, can you describe to me what kind of cough you're having? Is it like a wet cough or a dry cough? It's a dry cough. Okay, and has it been getting worse over the, over the time period you described? Um, it's kind of the same. It's the same. Um, have you noticed, although it's like dry, have you brought up any kind of sputum or phlegm at all? Um no, not, not, not recently. But yeah, in the first couple of days, the first two, three days. Okay, um and have you noticed any kind of yellowish to greenish tinge to it? No, it was watery. Watery, okay. Have you noticed at all, any blood, have you been coughing up any blood at all? Uh, no. Okay, and is your cough like constant or does it come and go? Um, kind of comes and goes. There's just times when I'm coughing a lot and then it just goes away. Okay, and is there a pattern to it? Like, uh, do you cough more when you're exerting yourself or any time, a certain time of the day? Um, it's worse when I'm smoking. When you're smoking, okay. And uh, how long do those episodes last? Couple of seconds, maybe? Okay, and has this happened to you before at all? Yeah. I think, um, yeah, I usually don't pay much attention to it. You know, I think must have happened last year or the year before. It just kind of goes away on its own. Okay, and is there any anything that makes it better or worse? Um, I um, I take this cough syrup, over the counter syrup, that helps. Okay. And um, have you found that it's been helping over, like, to help improve your cough over the last few days? It goes away for a couple hours and then it comes back. I see. Does it keep keep you up at night at all? No, no, no. No, okay. Are you having any other symptoms like shortness of breath, um sore throat, runny nose, anything like that? Uh, I kind of had those symptoms in those first two, three days, but then it went away, and then I only had this cough that stayed. Okay, okay. And have you been in contact with anyone that's been sick around you at all? Um, my daughter had the sniffles. When was that? Last, two weeks ago. Two weeks ago? Okay. And uh, how is she now like, did it just go away on its own, or did she have to go to the doctor? Um, no, it kind of went away on its own. Okay. Have you traveled at all recently outside of the province or the country? No. No, okay. And besides the cough, are you having any headaches at all? No. Any nausea or vomiting? No. Any fevers or chills? Not anymore. Not anymore, when was the last time that you had fevers or chills? I think I had like a very, it must have been 37.4, um, in the first, two, three days. But it got better really quickly. Okay, I see. Any night sweats? No. No, okay. Any, um, do you feel tired throughout the day? Not anymore. Not anymore, okay. So any kind of chest pain, or sensation that your heart is racing? No. No. Any dizziness or fainting spell? No. Any changes in your bowel movements at all? Um, no. No, so no constipation, diarrhea? No. Okay, any changes in your urination? No. No, okay. Um, have you had to miss work at all or, do you currently work? Yeah, but I've been working from home. Okay. I didn't have to miss work, so. Okay. And any changes in your sleep or appetite? No. Okay, any loss of sense of taste or smell at all? Um, initially, I guess the cough's first few days, my nose was a little stuffy, so yeah, I couldn't smell very well, but since then it's been fine. Okay, but no loss of taste? No loss of taste, no. Okay. Uh, any recent significant changes to your weight at all over the last few months? No. Okay, um, and any exposure in your home environment to dust, fumes? Any animals in the household? No. Okay. And otherwise, uh, do you have any medical conditions that you've been diagnosed with in the past? Uh no, I'm pretty healthy. Pretty healthy, okay. Any allergies, either to medications or environmental? Um, I'm allergic to penicillin. To penicillin, okay. And what kind of reaction do you get to penicillin? Um, I was told that I got a rash when I was a child. I don't really remember. Okay, and do you take any medications on a regular basis? No. No, okay. And have you been hospitalized in the past or had any surgeries? Um, I had my appendix removed about um, five years ago. Five years ago, okay. Any complications at all after that? No. Okay. Any family history of any lung conditions, heart conditions, any cancers at all? Um, colon cancer. Colon cancer? Yeah my dad and my grandfather had it. Okay, um, and have you, have you had any scopes done recently at all? Oh yeah, I had mine done uh, seven years ago. Seven years ago, okay. And were there any findings at all? No. No, okay. Um, currently so you work from home, so what's your home and living situation like? Who do you live with, and where, do you live in like a house, appartment? Uh, I live in an apartment with my wife and my daughter. Okay uh, and uh, do they have any symptoms at all? Or are they, are they fine? Well, my daughter doesn't have any symptoms anymore. She did as I mentioned, have some flu like symptoms 2 weeks ago. My wife's been healthy. Okay, and uh, does your daughter go to school right now or is she doing school from home? Uh, school from home. School from home, okay. Um, and do you at all smoke? So you mentioned your symptoms get worse with smoking. Yeah. Um, how much would you say you smoke on an average day? Um, I don't, I try, I've been trying to cut down, so I, I smoke about 3 or 4 cigarettes a week. I used to smoke a lot more before. Wow, yeah, that's really good! That's really great progress, um, it's really difficult to quit, but it's great to see that you're trying to quit. Um, so how much would you say you used to smoke? Well, half a pack. Um, half a pack a day, but then over the past three years I've been trying to cut it down. I started smoking when I was 20, so it's been um, 30 years now. Okay, so 30 years, okay. And um, are you just, are you using anything to help you quit smoking or are you doing it without any medications, or um, like, have you used anything in the past that has worked for you? Um, no I, I went to my doc and he said to try on my own first and if I'm struggling with it to come back, but I've been doing it very gradually so it's, it's going well. Okay, and that's really good. And yeah, if you, at all, need any resources to help you quit smoking, it's great that you're doing really well, but if you need to talk more about it or need any more resources we can provide that for you. Um, other than uh, smoking cigarettes, do you use any recreational drugs like marijuana at all? No. No. How about alcohol? Uh, I just drink on the weekends. Maybe a glass or two of wine. Okay, and uh yeah. So those were kind of all the questions I had for you today. Do you have any questions or concerns? No. No? I was, I guess I was just worried about the cough um, it's just, I was worried that it could be COVID. Yeah, so definitely right now, any of these symptoms, like COVID can have a lot of different symptoms um, so we should definitely make sure that we, to check if you have COVID or not and we'll get a COVID swab test done for you today, and the results will come back in 24 to 48 hours. Uh, and in that time, you will need to isolate even in your own home. Try to wear a mask around your family members, um, trying to eat separately and use all those methods, and, and then after the test comes back you may, you'll be contacted by public health if it's positive and kind of given more instruction on what to do. And it doesn't necessarily have to be COVID either, it can be just another virus uh, like it can be just your common cold that's been going on. So it does seem that your symptoms have gotten better. Oftentimes uh, a cough can be the last thing to go, it can take up to two weeks or more for the cough to get relieved, but it seems that you're not having any systemic symptoms like no fevers or anything else right now, so it's, it's uh, that's, that's pretty good. Great, I'm glad. Alright, yeah. Thank you. You're welcome. Take care.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presenting with dry cough for 1.5 weeks\n- Cough is intermittent, lasting a few seconds, and worse with smoking\n- Initially had sputum production (watery) and URI symptoms (sniffles, nasal congestion, decreased sense of smell) which resolved after 2-3 days\n- Cough is relieved temporarily by OTC cough syrup\n- Denies current fever, chills, night sweats, SOB, sore throat, rhinorrhea, headache, chest pain, palpitations, dizziness, syncope, N/V, changes in bowel movements or urination, fatigue, loss of taste or appetite, or unintentional weight changes\n- No missed work (working from home)\n- No recent travel\n- Daughter had flu-like illness 2 weeks ago which self-resolved\n- No known sick contacts\n- No exposure to dust, fumes, or animals\n\nPMHx:\n- Appendectomy 5 years ago without complications\n- Allergic to penicillin (childhood rash)\n\nFHx:\n- Colon cancer (father and paternal grandfather)\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives in apartment with wife and daughter\n- Smoking: currently 3-4 cigarettes/week, previously 0.5 PPD for 30 years (since age 20), gradually cutting down over past 3 years\n- Alcohol: 1-2 glasses of wine on weekends\n- Denies recreational drug use\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals signs: not provided\n- Physical examination: not provided\n- Investigations: not provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely post-viral cough\n- COVID-19 testing pending\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 swab\n- Self-isolation and infection control measures at home pending swab results\n- Monitor symptoms and follow up if worsening or persistent cough"}
{"ID": "soap_00053", "dialogue": "What do you do? I am a registered nurse. That's nice. And do you smoke or drink? No sir. Any history of drug abuse or anything? Absolutely not! Okay, good.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is a registered nurse.\n- No history of smoking or drinking.\n- No history of drug abuse.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00084", "dialogue": "Hi there, how are you dear? I am fine, thank you. Guest_family: Hi Doctor, I am her mother. Nice to meet you. So I see you have been referred by Doctor X. Guest_family: Yes, she is our family doctor. Okay, so you noticed some behavioral problems with your daughter and reported to her, is that right? Guest_family: Yes, I have been noticing them for a while. She loses her focus quickly. We also got a few complaints from school regarding her behavior. We also met a couple of other doctors but they diagnosed her with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder. Well, looking at her reports I am afraid it looks like she does have A D H D, but the good news is we can treat her, so don't worry. Guest_family: Okay. I am going to prescribe her some medicines which will help her deal with this. Guest_family: Thank you. So, I will write her a prescription for Adderall and Clonidine. They have some really good results in treating A D H D. Which pharmacy do you go to? Guest_family: We go to Costco pharmacy by the X Y Z corner. Okay, I will send it there. Thank you doctor, it was nice meeting you. Nice meeting you too, take care.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Behavioral problems, quick loss of focus.\n- Severity: Not explicitly stated, but implied to be significant enough to warrant concern.\n- Duration: Not specified, but mentioned as having been noticed for a while.\n- Associated symptoms: Complaints from school regarding behavior.\n- Relevant medical history: Diagnosed with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by other doctors.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: Concern from the mother about the daughter's behavior and diagnosis.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Diagnosis: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD).\n- Clinical impression: The physician confirms the diagnosis based on the reports and suggests that treatment is possible.\n\nPlan:\n- Medications prescribed: Adderall and Clonidine.\n- Pharmacy: Prescription to be sent to Costco pharmacy by the X Y Z corner.\n- Follow-up: Not explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00096", "dialogue": "Are you aware of any medical problems in your family? No. Anyone with any heart disease or diabetes? No.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No medical problems reported in the family.\n- No family history of heart disease or diabetes.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data mentioned.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessments or diagnoses provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00003", "dialogue": "How may I help you today? Yeah I've had, a fever and a cough that started just like four days ago. OK, when did you, when did you start having your, so the cough started four days ago, what about the fever? I think, like, last night was when I checked, it was high, like over thirty eight. I see, OK. And, did you check your fever before that? The night before? Yeah, I just checked it last night. I felt hot previously, but, I don't know, I thought I was going to just get better, but this morning still, well, feeling a bit a little bit worse. OK, and the cough that started four days ago, has it been a dry cough or wet cough? It's been dry. OK, so there's been no sputum or any, or blood? No sputum or blood OK. Has it gotten worse over the past four days? Uh, a little bit, yeah. Uhm, yes. We do, OK. Any muscle aches? Uh, no. OK, Sore throat? No. Any difficulties breathing? No. Any wheezing? No. Any chest pain or pressure? No. OK, any runny nose? Yeah, my nose has been a little bit runny. OK, that started over the past four days as well? Yeah. OK, what about pain in your belly? No pain. OK, any diarrhea or Constipation? Uhm, no. Any nausea or vomitting? No. Any urinary symptoms? No. OK, any rash that you've noticed on your skin? No. Any changes in your sense of taste or your sense of smell? Uh, yeah, I haven't been able to smell as well. You feel like you have a runny nose, but is it sort of congested? Not really, it, yeah, just kind of running, and yeah, I haven't been able to smell very well. OK, and do you have any past medical, any conditions, medical conditions? Uh, depression and anxiety. And have you had any surgeries in the past or hospitalizations? Uhm, no OK, are you taking any medications, including over the counter pills? I do take a multi-vitamin. And, uh. sorry I do take, um, I take, uh, an SSRI, Citalopram as well. OK, that's good. OK, any allergies? No allergies OK, specifically allergies to medications? Uhm, yeah no, no medications, I don't think so. Good, ok, and do you currently smoke or have you ever smoked in the past? Uhm, I smoked when I was in high school very briefly, but I haven't smoked anything in the last twenty years. Alright, do you consume marijuana? Uh, we'll use edibles every once in a while. I buy them from the, like, I guess government store, OCS. I see, OK, any recreational drugs? No, OK, and do you currently live alone or do you live with others? I live with others, I live with my girlfriend, and a dog. OK, and has she been sick or have you been in contact with anyone who's been sick recently? Uh, she just came back from a work trip. I've been working from home but she was, uhm, in Saskatchewan, and just got back last week. OK, and has she had any symptoms? She's been fatigued since coming back, but I mean, we thought that was maybe just from the travels, but, maybe not. Has she been quarantining, or are you both spending time together? Well, we've been, I mean, trying to distance in the home, but I mean, we've been living together in an apartment so. Fair enough, fair enough. OK, have you had any travels? No, I've been working from home. OK, alright, and uh, any, you don't have any asthma or COPD or any lung conditions, right? Right, I don't have anything like that. OK, and, any conditions that run in your family? Uhm, no, nothing that runs in the family. Alright, OK, so if I understand correctly, you've been feeling tired and you've had a cough and a runny nose and changes in your sense of smell for the past three or four days and you also were feeling a little bit hot and yesterday you noticed that you had spiked a high fever. Is there anything that I'm missing? No, I think that covers all the symptoms. And you might have potentially had, some exposure through your girlfriend potentially, so it's possible that, well, it's possible that you just have a cold, but it's also, we'll still have to rule out covid as a cause, and so we'll have to do a covid swab and go from there and we'll check your temperature here as well. Just, uh, I think something that I forgot to ask, have you taken any medications that has helped you with your symptoms? Uh no, I haven't taken anything. OK, alright, well, thank you, I'll go talk to my attending and we'll come back and discuss this further with you. That sounds great. Thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Fever and dry cough started 4 days ago\n- Fever noted to be high (>38\u00b0C) last night\n- Symptoms worsened slightly over the past 4 days\n- Associated symptoms: fatigue, runny nose, anosmia\n- No sputum, blood, muscle aches, sore throat, dyspnoea, wheezing, chest pain, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, constipation, nausea, vomiting, urinary symptoms, rash\n- No recent travel; girlfriend recently returned from a work trip and has been fatigued\n\nPast Medical History:\n- PMHx: depression, anxiety\n- Medications: multivitamin, citalopram (SSRI)\n- No known drug allergies\n- SocHx: former smoker (high school), occasional marijuana edibles, lives with girlfriend and dog\n- No significant FHx\n\nObjective:\n- Temperature to be checked\n\nAssessment:\n- Possible viral infection (cold or COVID-19)\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 swab\n- Monitor temperature and symptoms"}
{"ID": "soap_00086", "dialogue": "Good afternoon Sir. Do you know how you got here? Yes, I passed out and the paramedics brought me to the hospital. Do you have any recollection of the incidences leading up to your loss of consciousness? I think I had a seizure and then I passed out. Prior to the episode did you experience any chest pain or shortness of breath? No. Any feelings of abnormal heart rhythm? No. Do you have any history of any cardiac problems? No. How are you feeling now? I feel fine. Do you know how old you are? I am sixty nine.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Loss of consciousness, possible seizure.\n- Severity: Not specified.\n- Duration: Not specified.\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned prior to the episode; no chest pain or shortness of breath.\n- Relevant medical history: No history of cardiac problems.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: Patient feels fine now.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00015", "dialogue": "So, I am looking at your reports, you don't have anemia or any other blood disorders. Well, that's a relief! Yeah. Let me check your lymph nodes. They are nonpalpable. I don't see any swollen glands or anything. Great!", "note": "Subjective:\n- The patient expresses relief about not having anemia or any other blood disorders.\n\nObjective:\n- Lymph nodes are nonpalpable.\n- No swollen glands observed.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment information provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan information provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00036", "dialogue": "Do you have a family history of any medical conditions, such as diabetes or heart disease? No, nothing.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient has no family history of medical conditions, such as diabetes or heart disease.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00037", "dialogue": "I will ask some simple questions, okay? Hm How old are you and how would you define yourself in terms of ethnicity? Well, I am forty seven and would proudly call myself as Latin American. Great. What brings you to the emergency room? I had a blood test before and then it came out to be high potassium. I also went to a regular clinic the other day as I was feeling very weak and extremely tired. Okay. My potassium came out to be seven and it was highlighted in red. That really scared me, so I wanted to know everything what's going on inside me. I told the emergency doctors too that all these weakness and tiredness is going on for more than half a year. Did you noticed any other symptoms other than weakness? Yes, there was the fever and chills. I was sweating a lot in the nights, and I also had weight loss of around twenty pounds. During that time, I sometimes have nausea and was throwing up a lot. I was not eating. I always felt dizzy every time I tried to stand. Did you have wet cough? No, not wet cough but I did notice back pain gets intense on the upper side whenever I coughed, like in my ribs. I normally massage that area and it gets better. Any shortness of breath? Nope. How about while sleeping or doing something else? Not while sleeping, but I did notice that if I'm walking for two to three blocks then I get out of breath easily. Previously it was not like this. I used to jog for two to three miles without even a single breath loss. I noticed one more thing. I feel like my balls on one side are getting big. Which side left or right? I think its left. For how long? I guess over the past year. I did see someone when all of this started, and he put me on some antibiotics, but nothing changed. For last few months there is yellow fluid coming out of it too. Did you ever get any type of injury in the area? No. What about your bowel movements? Nope everything is as usual. Any change in urinary habits? Nope. Any changes in your skin? Any rash? Any kind of eyesight issue? Nope, no changes. What about pain? Like headache or any joint pain? Nope, no pain anywhere.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Weakness, extreme tiredness, fever, chills, night sweats, weight loss (around 20 pounds), nausea, vomiting, dizziness when standing, enlargement of left testicle with yellow fluid discharge.\n- Severity: Potassium level of 7 (high), weakness and tiredness ongoing for more than half a year.\n- Duration: Symptoms have been present for over six months; testicle symptoms for about a year.\n- Associated symptoms: Fever, chills, night sweats, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, shortness of breath with exertion.\n- Relevant medical history: Previous antibiotic treatment with no improvement.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: High potassium level and associated symptoms causing significant concern.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data mentioned.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00060", "dialogue": "Do you have any medical conditions that I should know about? Um, I have high blood pressure, and asthma. I also see here that you have cervical cancer, can you tell me more about that? Yeah, I got diagnosed at fifteen years old. How would you describe the progression of your cancer? Now, um, I'd say it's dormant.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: High blood pressure, asthma\n- Medical History: Diagnosed with cervical cancer at fifteen years old\n- Current Status of Cancer: Describes cancer as dormant\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00045", "dialogue": "I was wondering if you could start by telling me what brought you into the emergency Department. So I came to the emergency Department because I've been having pain in my chest. OK, where is the pain located? I would say it's like the whole pretty much the left side of my chest. OK, and when did this pain start? So I think it started just around 2 hours ago. I actually was riding my bicycle and just fell off on, kind of just slipped and I think I fell on that side like when I the impact was right on my chest and since then it's been really painful. OK I am how would you describe the pain, sometimes people will use words like burning dull, achy. I think it's more like someone is just stabbing me and then whole left side area. OK, and um. Anything that you've been doing that's that's made the pain worse? If I. Think I have to take really really slow and shallow breaths. If I try to take a deep breath it just really hurts like taking those small, smaller breaths helps. OK, and do you feel the pain anywhere else, or is it just on the left side of your chest? No, I would say just on the left side. OK, and has the pain been getting worse, staying the same or getting better? I'd say over the last two hours it's been getting worse. OK, and do you have any other associated symptoms? Uh, like I think I I'm having. Also maybe it's just because I'm trying to breathe slowly, but I feel like I can't catch my breath. OK, um has anything like this ever happened to you before? No, no. I mean, I've never had any pain in my chest before. OK are you, did you have a loss of consciousness when you fell off your bike? No, I got right back up. I just just had the pain. OK, so any injuries to the head? No. OK. That's fine. I was wearing a helmet. OK, excellent and any headache right now? No. OK, how about any fever chills? No, I feel I'm fine. Any nausea or vomiting? Nope. Do you feel as though your heart's racing? I would say like in the last hour a little bit more so than usual. OK. Any skin changes or or rashes you've noticed? Besides, just I got some scrapes here and there on my hands, but nothing else. OK, and do you have a cough? Nope. OK, and any recent infections? No, I've been pretty pretty healthy recently. No, no cough or no infections. OK, and uh, any dizziness? Um no. Alright, and I know you mentioned the few scratches on your hands, but any other injuries? Oh no, I I think I got a pretty lucky. Oh, OK. And any alcohol or drug intake prior to falling off your bike? Oh no, no, I was completely sober. No no drugs or alcohol. OK, and any weakness or numbness or tingling that you noticed? Um no nothing like that. Alright, and, when was the last time you had anything to eat or drink? I just had some cereal and, um, just some chocolate milk around breakfast time but nothing else. OK, and how many hours ago was that? I would say around 3 hours ago. OK. Um, and have you been diagnosed with any medical conditions in the past? Uh, not other than just a hay fever, but nothing nothing else. I have like a lot of allergies. Any allergies to medications? Oh, just sometimes when I take like a nasal spray I think I might have an allergy to the steroids that they give me and sometimes also some of the antihistamines I think I get some allergies from them. OK. Uh, and any hospitalizations? No, never. Any surgeries? Nope. OK. Anybody in the family who have any illnesses? Just my father. He has type 2 diabetes but nothing else. OK, and how do you support yourself currently financially? Oh so I work just as like a mechanic just in the city and currently I just have myself to support. I live alone in an apartment, so I'm doing fairly well. OK, and do you smoke? I smoke around like I'd say 2 and a half to 3 packs per day. I know it's. Yeah, I have tried quitting before, but I would say I've been smoking since I was 18. OK, and do you smoke marijuana? No, no, no marijuana, no other drugs. OK, and do you drink alcohol? Yeah, it's just like sometimes on the weekend. I'll just have a beer or two while watching the game, but not nothing regular. OK, thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Chest pain on the left side\n- Started 2 hours ago after falling off a bicycle\n- Described as stabbing pain\n- Worsens with deep breaths, alleviated by shallow breaths\n- Pain has been getting worse over the last 2 hours\n- Associated symptoms: shortness of breath, heart racing\n- No previous episodes of chest pain\n- No loss of consciousness, no head injury, no headache, no fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, cough, dizziness, weakness, numbness, or tingling\n- No recent infections\n- Scrapes on hands from the fall\n- No alcohol or drug intake prior to the fall\n\nPast Medical History:\n- PMHx: hay fever, allergies to nasal spray steroids and some antihistamines\n- No hospitalisations or surgeries\n- FHx: father with type 2 diabetes\n- SocHx: works as a mechanic, lives alone, smokes 2.5-3 packs/day, occasional alcohol on weekends, no marijuana or other drugs\n\nObjective:\n- No vitals or physical examination findings provided\n\nAssessment:\n- No diagnosis or differential diagnosis provided\n\nPlan:\n- No investigations or treatment plans provided"}
{"ID": "soap_00023", "dialogue": "How's your asthma since you started using your inhaler again? Much better. I don't know why I didn't take it with me everywhere I went. It's important to carry it with you, especially during times where you're exercising or walking more than usual. Yeah. I think I've learned my lesson. Besides asthma, do you have any other medical problems?", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Asthma\n- Severity: Much better since using inhaler\n- Duration: Not specified\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: Asthma\n- Family history: Not mentioned\n- Allergies: Not mentioned\n- Other concerns: Patient acknowledges the importance of carrying the inhaler during exercise and walking.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment information provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Emphasize the importance of carrying the inhaler during exercise or increased physical activity."}
{"ID": "soap_00009", "dialogue": "What brings you in today? Yeah, so I've had this cough that started within the last day and uhm, I've also been particularly short of breath as well. OK. Can you tell me about the, the cough, how, is it dry cough or wet cough? It's wet, I've been bringing up, uh, sputum that's uh, It's just, it leaves a really bad taste in my mouth when bringing it up. What color is the sputum? It's kind of, uh, like, yellowish green, type color. Havey you noticed any blood? No, I haven't seen any blood. And it only started yesterday. What about the difficulties breathing, when did that start? Yeah, that started really within the last twelve hours or so. And can you tell me if it's gotten better or worse since then? Yeah, it's been, it's been getting worse, uhm, and the phlegm, also smells really bad. OK. When do you have difficult, like, is there anything that makes the, shortness of breath, worse? Yeah, if I, uhm, when I was having a cigarette earlier today, it got a bit worse and when I went out to get the Mail today I, I just couldn't do it. I, I had to just sit down. And that what happened to you before, right? OK. Uh, and say two days ago, were you feeling perfectly fine? I was yeah, I wasn't sick at all. OK, so you've had this bad cough and you've had some difficulties breathing since yesterday. Have you had a headache at all? No, no headache. Any, uh, runny nose? No, it's really, no. Any congestion? No. No, OK. Have you had any pain in your chest? Uh, no pain. When you're breathing in or breathing out any pain at that time? or deep breathing? No, I haven't had any pain. OK, have you had any palpitations or you feel like your heart's racing? Uh, yeah, I do. I feel like my heart has been beating a little bit faster. OK. And any pain in your belly? No. Nausea or vomiting? Uh no, but, well I guess I have felt a little nauseous with the, uhm, bad taste in my mouth. OK, and any uh diarrhea or Constipation? Uhm no, I haven't anything like that. OK, any sore throat? Uhm, no. OK alright uhm, any other symptoms that I haven't talked about, that you experienced over the past day? No, it's really been, uhm, yeah, this cough and bringing up this, this sputum and being short of breath. OK, uhm, can you tell me about your past uh, medical history, do you have any medical conditions? Uh yeah, I have uhm, high blood pressure. Mhm. I also have acid reflux, so uh, gerd I think, think it's called? Yeah. And, uhm, high cholesterol as well. OK, ok. And I've also been, had issues with anxiety in the past as well. In terms of medications, are you taking any? Yeah, I take uhm, an ACE inhibitor, statin, uhm, and uh, medication for my stomach, I think, like Pantoprazole. OK, and uh, how well has your gerd been controlled? Uh, it kind of, it bugs me most days, its not so bad, I mean, sometimes I'll get a little bit of heartburn and acid in the, in my mouth, but it depends on what I eat so I really try to stay away from some, some foods. So the PPI hasn't completely curbed the, the acid reflux, you still get it? Yeah, I still get it. It's definitely helped though it was a lot worse before, uh starting it. I've been on been on it now for a few years. OK, and as for your anxiety, do you ever get panic attacks? No, I haven't had any, any panic attacks. OK, alright. Do you have any allergies? No. OK, so you said that these symptoms started yesterday and they were quite sudden, uh, was there anything that you were doing yesterday or the day before that was different or unusual? Well, yeah, I mean yesterday I, or, yeah, I had a bit more to drink than, uh, usual, more alcohol I mean. So, I mean, could that have something to do with it? I'm not sure. It could potentially. Did you, were you, were you drunk and did you pass out? Yeah, I think so. My, my friends, uh yeah, that's what my friends were, were saying anyway. OK, and uh, talking about alcohol, how much do you usually drink? I usually, like, like four to six beers a day, but uh, yeah, yesterday was certainly more than that. Have you considered reducing the amount of alcohol you drink? Has it affected you in any way? I mean I, I'm retired now so, uhm, I don't know, I, I feel like it's been fine maybe, uh, I feel like it's been fine. OK, OK, I suppose we could talk a little bit more about that later if you want uhm, as four to six beers is quite a lot actually, and it can affect your health in certain ways, but we can, we can chat more about that later. OK. And, uh, do you smoke? Yeah, I smoke, uhm, a half a pack of cigarettes per day. It was well over a pack before, so I've cut down a little bit. No, that's great. OK, do you plan on cutting it down further or do you wanna stay right there? Yeah I have been, have been trying, just too, too darn expensive these days, so, been trying to cut back. Yeah, yeah, that's fair, alright. What about marijuana? Do you consume marijuana? No, I don't. Any recreational drugs? Nope. Alright, do you have any family history of any medical problems? Uh yeah, my, my father had, uh, he ended up having liver disease actually, he passed away from that I think. And he had heart problems too. He was a lifelong smoker and didn't really, didn't really take care of himself. Hmm, I see. Do you, do you have a family doctor? Uh, I do. I haven't seen my family doctor in a number of years now though. Yeah, well, you know it's, it's a good idea to see your family doctor on a regular basis, specially when you're getting older, because sometimes you might need changes in your medications, or there might be checkups or screenings that they might want to do. It's for your own benefit, you know, maybe even just seeing them once a year would be a good idea. I would definitely recommend that you book an appointment with them just for a checkup in the next couple of weeks. Is there a reason why you don't see your family doctor too often? I just haven't been, uhm, sick and I've been able to, to refill my prescriptions, mostly from just calling in, so. OK, ok, I see, uhm, alright, and do you live alone or do you live with other people? Yeah, I live with my wife. OK, alright, and are you able to take care of things like groceries or finances, or shoveling snow or cleaning the house, or do you need any assistance with those sort of household activities? Yeah, we moved, uhm, actually into an apartment last year. It's made it a lot easier in terms of the housework and that sort of thing. But I've been able to, uhm, I do the the grocery shopping and share the the chores around, around the house. I do, I'll use a cane sometimes. I, I injured my knee a number of years ago, but other than that, things are OK. OK, OK, good to hear. That reminds me, have you had any surgeries in the past? Just on my, my left knee. I had a left knee replacement a few years ago. OK alright and have you any had, had any hospitalizations? No. OK, alright, uhm, so sounds like you've had a day of this wet cough with the, this, a bad smelling sort of sputum that's, that's yellowish green in color, and you've also had difficulties breathing for the past twelve hours, uh, so much so that you can't even go out to get mail and your hearts been racing. It's possible that while you were drunk that you might have, and given that you have acid reflux, that you might have accidentally have something go inside your breathing tube from, from your mouth or something that came up from your stomach and sometimes when that happens, food items can or other things can get lodged in your lungs and can become, uhm, can attract bacteria to, uh, to replicate and cause a bacterial infection, so it's possible that, that's what happened But it could also be a viral pneumonia or a viral infection in your chest. we'll get a chest x-ray done, we'll have some labs done as well, and we'll likely start some antibiotics. Have you recently been on any antibiotics? No, I haven't. And you don't have any allergies to any, correct? Correct. Alright, OK, we'll also do a physical exam just to give a listen to your, to your lungs and see how they're doing and then go from there. Do you have any questions for me? No, that sounds good. Thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presenting with cough that started yesterday, wet cough with yellowish-green sputum that smells bad and leaves a bad taste in mouth\n- Shortness of breath started within last 12 hours, has been worsening\n- Shortness of breath worse with smoking cigarettes and going out to get mail, had to sit down due to inability to get mail which is unusual\n- Feels heart has been beating a little faster\n- Felt a little nauseous with the bad taste in mouth\n- No headache, runny nose, congestion, chest pain, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, or sore throat\n\nPMHx:\n- Hypertension\n- GERD - still gets heartburn and acid in mouth at times despite PPI, worse with certain foods\n- Hyperlipidemia\n- Anxiety - no panic attacks\n- Left knee replacement a few years ago\n\nMedications: ACE inhibitor, statin, pantoprazole\n\nSocHx:\n- Retired\n- Drinks 4-6 beers daily, had more than usual yesterday and friends reported he passed out drunk\n- Smokes 0.5 PPD cigarettes, down from 1 PPD previously\n- Denies marijuana or recreational drug use\n- Lives with wife in apartment, able to do grocery shopping and household chores, uses cane at times due to previous knee injury\n\nFHx:\n- Father had liver disease (passed away from this) and heart problems, was a lifelong smoker\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals signs\n- Respiratory examination\n\nAssessment:\n- Possible aspiration pneumonia or viral pneumonia/infection\n\nPlan:\n- Chest X-ray\n- Labs\n- Likely start antibiotics\n- Recommend follow-up with PCP for regular check-ups, medication adjustments, and age-appropriate screenings"}
{"ID": "soap_00012", "dialogue": "So let's see here. Any history of high blood pressure or diabetes? No, sir. How about heart disease? No, no heart disease. And no cancers? No and for that I'm thankful.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No history of high blood pressure.\n- No history of diabetes.\n- No history of heart disease.\n- No history of cancers.\n- Patient expresses gratitude for not having cancer.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided]"}
{"ID": "soap_00039", "dialogue": "Guest_clinician: How is the patient's condition now? Well, he has improved clinically but would require acute care. Guest_clinician: Oh, I see.", "note": "Subjective:\n- The patient has improved clinically but requires acute care.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided)\n\nAssessment:\n- The patient's condition has improved, but acute care is still needed.\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00070", "dialogue": "Guest_clinician: Hello, doctor. Let me give you a run down as to what we are dealing with today. This patient was last seen in January two thousand and four. She is coming for a follow up on interstitial disease secondary to lupus pneumonitis. I will let her tell you more about what happened yesterday. Thanks, nurse. How are you feeling today, miss? Welcome back to the Pulmonary Medicine Clinic. What happened yesterday? Well, I was doing fine since last time. My respiratory issues have been great and I have had no major difficulties breathing. That's good to hear. What happened yesterday that caused you to want to come in so urgently? Yesterday I noticed that I have some back and joint pain. When I breathe deep my back hurts. Are you experiencing any coughing? No, coughing at this moment. Have you been producing any mucus the times that you do cough? No, I already said I didn't cough. Okay. Any fevers or chills? Nothing so far. Do you have any other symptoms currently? I do notice that I am more tired than usual. During physical activity do you seem out of breath or more tired than usual? I want to see if your pulmonary system is inhibited. No. I don't have issues with breathing when I do daily activities.", "note": "Initial:\n\nSubjective:\n- Symptoms: Back pain, joint pain, increased fatigue.\n- Severity: Not specified.\n- Duration: Symptoms noted yesterday.\n- Associated symptoms: No cough, no mucus production, no fever or chills.\n- Relevant medical history: Interstitial disease secondary to lupus pneumonitis.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Concerns: Patient is feeling more tired than usual but does not have breathing issues during daily activities.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00098", "dialogue": "How are you doing today, ma'am? I'm doing just fine. Any kind of alcohol consumption? Nope. Have you ever taken any kind of illegal drugs? Nope. What about smoking? No. Are you still working? No, sir. I had issues with my knees, and they had to put in the new ones. I was kind of disabled and that's why I retired.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Issues with knees.\n- Severity: Not specified.\n- Duration: Not specified.\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned.\n- Relevant medical history: Knee issues; had new knees put in; retired due to disability.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: None mentioned.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00092", "dialogue": "So, to double check, you have a history of hay fever and asthma, correct? Yes. I was also recently diagnosed with cervical dysplasia. I found out shortly after my last O B G Y N visit. And are you planning to follow up with your O B G Y N in the upcoming months? Yeah, the next visit is scheduled and everything.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: History of hay fever and asthma; recently diagnosed with cervical dysplasia.\n- Duration: Recent diagnosis of cervical dysplasia (specific time not mentioned).\n- Relevant medical history: History of hay fever and asthma; cervical dysplasia diagnosed shortly after the last OB-GYN visit.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Concerns: Planning to follow up with OB-GYN in the upcoming months; next visit is scheduled.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Follow-up scheduled with OB-GYN in the upcoming months."}
{"ID": "soap_00073", "dialogue": "What brings you in here today? Yeah, I have this pain in my chest. OK, and where is the pain exactly? It's just right over on the on the left side. OK, and when did this pain start? It started just 30 minutes ago. OK, and did it just come on randomly or were you doing something strenuous? I was just shovelling the driveway and it came on. OK, and has that pain been getting worse at all over the last half an hour? No, it just came on suddenly and it's uh, uh, I'm sorry. Yeah the pain has been there this whole time and it's gotten worse ever since it started. OK, and how would you describe the pain? Is it kind of like an aching pain or is it a sharp or tight tightness kind of pain? How would you describe it? It feels dull. I feel like there's a lot of pressure on my chest. And how do you rate the pain right now on a scale of zero to 10, zero being the least amount of pain you felt in your life, 10 being the worst? Uh, seven. Seven, OK. Have you had ny similar episodes before? No, I've never had any chest pain before. OK, and is the pain just staying in the region of the left chest area that you mentioned or is it traveling to any other part of your body? No, I'm kind of just feeling it right here. On the left side. OK, uh, is there anything that you do that makes the pain either get worse or go away or like get better? Uh. I think it's a bit bit worse if I'm moving around or when I was walking in here. I think it it made it a bit worse, but nothing has seemed to make it any better since it starting. OK, and does it change at all from you changing positions like if you're standing up versus sitting down or laying down? I think it's a little bit worse when I when I'm laying down. OK, and other than the pain that you've been having, have you been having any other symptoms like a cough or difficulty breathing or any pain when you're breathing in or out? Uh, I've felt a little bit uh short of breath or having difficulty breathing since yesterday when the sorry since the pain started, but uh just the difficulty breathing. OK. And have you recently injured your chest or surrounding area at all? Like from a fall or anything like that? I do I play rugby and was tackled by another player yesterday. But but my chest felt fine after that. OK so but the pain just started half an hour ago? Yeah. OK, have you have you been traveling at all recently? No, been at home. OK, has anyone around you been sick at all? No. OK, have you been having any symptoms like nausea or vomiting or any fevers or chills? No nausea or vomiting, but I do feel a little bit hot today. OK, but have you measured your temperature at all? Uh I did and it was 38 degrees. OK, and have you been having any kind of swelling in your legs or feet? Uh, no swelling in my legs. OK. Have you been feeling tired at all, like increasingly fatigued? No, my energy has been good. It uhh it does feel like it's beating faster right now. It usually only feels like this when I'm playing sports. OK. And have you noticed any changes in your skin at all? Any rashes? No rashes. OK, have you had any cough or runny nose or sore throat? Any kind of those symptoms and in the past month? Uh few weeks ago I was a little runny, but that went away on its own. I haven't had any cough. OK, and have you been feeling dizzy at all or have you fainted? No dizziness and uh no I haven't fainted at all. OK. Just a few more questions. Have you had any diagnosis made by any physician or any anything like diabetes or high blood pressure? Yeah, I've been told I have high cholesterol and high blood pressure. OK, and do you take any medications for the these things? Um, I do take medications for both blood pressure and cholesterol, Rosuvastatin and um Lisinopril and I take a multi vitamin. OK, and do you have any allergies to any medications at all? No allergies. OK, have you at all in the past been hospitalized for any reason? No hospitalizations. Any previous surgeries? No. OK, and within your family, has anyone passed away from a heart attack or any cancers that run in the family? No. OK, and currently right now, do you live alone? Do you live with someone? And where do you live, like an apartment or house? I I live in a house with um, my parents. OK, and do you currently work? Yeah, I I drive a bus for the city. OK. And in your daily routine, would you say you get enough exercise throughout the week? Uhh yeah, usually on Sundays I'll go for a run. OK, and how about your diet? Well, how's your diet like, just regularly? Uhh, usually I feel like it's fairly balanced overall, I might I eat out a little bit too often, but try to eat as many vegetables as I can. OK. And do you smoke cigarettes at all? I do yes. Been smoking for the last 20 years roughly. OK, how much do you smoke on an average day? About a half a pack to a pack a day. OK. And do you drink? No alcohol. OK, and any recreational drugs like marijuana? Um, no marijuana, but I have used crystal meth in the past. OK, and when was the last time that you used crystal meth? Um, six days ago. Six days ago, OK, and how often do you use crystal meth? Um, I would say a couple of times a month. A couple of times a month, OK. Alright, and for how long have you been using crystal meth? Uh for the last seven years. Seven years, OK.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Chest pain (left side)\n- Duration: 30 minutes\n- Onset: While shoveling driveway\n- Progression: Worsening since onset\n- Quality: Dull, pressure-like\n- Severity: 7/10\n- Aggravating factors: Movement, walking, laying down\n- Alleviating factors: None\n- Associated symptoms: SOB, feeling hot (38\u00b0C), palpitations\n- No previous episodes of chest pain\n- No radiation of pain\n- No recent travel\n- No recent illness exposure\n- No nausea, vomiting, fever, chills, leg swelling, fatigue, dizziness, fainting, cough, or rashes\n- PMHx: High cholesterol, high blood pressure\n- Medications: Rosuvastatin, lisinopril, multivitamin\n- No allergies\n- No hospitalizations or surgeries\n- FHx: No heart attacks or cancers\n- SocHx: Lives with parents, bus driver, exercises weekly, balanced diet, smokes (20 years, 0.5-1 pack/day), no alcohol, past crystal meth use (last use 6 days ago, 7 years, couple of times/month)\n\nPast Medical History:\n- High cholesterol\n- High blood pressure\n- No hospitalizations\n- No surgeries\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals: Not provided\n- Physical exam: Not provided\n- Investigations with results: Not provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Not provided\n\nPlan:\n- Not provided"}
{"ID": "soap_00080", "dialogue": "They gave you Procrit for your anemia? Yes I think so. That is how they treated it in the past. Okay. Are you married? Who do you live with? Oh I am by myself. Okay, and do you smoke or drink alcohol? No I do not. Great. Okay.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient has anemia.\n- Patient was treated with Procrit in the past for anemia.\n- Patient lives alone.\n- Patient does not smoke or drink alcohol.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00016", "dialogue": "Hello, miss. How can I help you today? The nurse said the matter was urgent. Yes, I have been having dizzy spells. I have also noticed a new rash on my right calf. Can you describe what type of dizzy spells you are having? What do they feel like? It is general dizziness and vertigo. I am also lightheaded during the spells. Are you feeling okay right now or are you experiencing symptoms? I am having headaches off and on randomly, but nothing right now. The neck pain and muscle aches also are intermittent. Let me take a look at the rash on your legs. Are the bruises new? They are new, I think. I have noticed them more than usual. In the past couple days have you had a fever or chills? No, nothing like that. What about chest pains? I have had some tightness in my chest, but not right now. Does the dizziness make you nauseous? Yes, but I have not had to throw up or anything. What about diarrhea? No, thank goodness. Are there any parts of your body that are hurting? I do have some left shoulder pain. Not so much painful, but it is uncomfortable and tender. Have you measured your blood sugar at all? I ask because the notes state there is a history of gestational diabetes and when blood sugars are out of the normal limits this can cause some of the symptoms you are having. I measured it today. After lunch it was one fifty five.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Dizziness (general dizziness and vertigo), lightheadedness, headaches (off and on), neck pain (intermittent), muscle aches (intermittent), tightness in the chest (not currently), discomfort and tenderness in the left shoulder, new rash on the right calf, new bruises (more than usual).\n- Severity: Lightheadedness, symptoms of dizziness are concerning.\n- Duration: Dizziness spells and other symptoms are ongoing; headaches are random; rash and bruises noted in the past couple of days.\n- Associated symptoms: Nausea associated with dizziness (but no vomiting), no diarrhea, no fever or chills.\n- Relevant medical history: History of gestational diabetes.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: The patient expresses concern regarding the dizziness, headaches, and the new rash.\n\nObjective:\n- Blood sugar measurement: 155 mg/dL after lunch.\n- Examination findings: New rash on the right calf, new bruises noted.\n\nAssessment:\n- No formal assessment or diagnosis provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Further evaluation of dizziness and rash needed.\n- Monitor blood sugar levels due to history of gestational diabetes."}
{"ID": "soap_00064", "dialogue": "How're you feeling today? Much better. The antibiotics seemed to really help. Are you still experiencing any symptoms? Um no, thankfully. I had some wheezing when I first started the antibiotics, but it's improved a ton. I don't think the wheeze is even there anymore. Good, good.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Wheezing\n- Severity: Improved significantly\n- Duration: Initially present when starting antibiotics\n- Associated Symptoms: None mentioned\n- Relevant Medical History: None mentioned\n- Family History: None mentioned\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other Concerns: Patient feels much better; antibiotics seemed to help.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00029", "dialogue": "What brings you in here today? I've just been feeling really short of breath since this morning, and I don't, uh yeah I don't know what's going on. It just started this morning. Yeah. And uh, did you wake up feeling short of breath or was it after you had woken up and we were doing activities? It was when I was doing activities. OK, and what were you doing before you had the shortness of breath? Oh, I was just going to the kitchen to make breakfast. OK, and uhm can you describe the shortness of breath? Is it more so that you're having trouble, deep breaths, or are you taking shallow fast breaths? How would you describe it? Uhm? I guess just shallow, fast breaths, I feel like I've run a marathon and I just yeah and all I've done is just walked to the kitchen. OK and has this happened to you before ever? No. No? OK. Are you having any other symptoms like off sore throat, chest pain, anything like that? No. So it's just the shortness of breath. Yeah, that's it. OK. And so it it started this morning, is it still continuing, or is it, uh? Still there. OK, is it worse at all than it was this morning? Uhm, kind of the same, I'd say. OK, are you getting any kind of chest discomfort or anything at all with breathing? Maybe when I take a very deep breath, then yeah. It just kind of I feel like this sharp pain. OK. How have you, having I've been having any chills or fever or anything like that? No. OK, any dizziness or fainting spells? No. OK, do you feel that your heart is racing at all? No. OK, And, and have you tried anything that's helped you improve those symptoms, like taking a rest or laying down flat? Anything like that? No, haven't really tried much. It just started this morning so I I just decided come in. Right away. Ok. Have you traveled at all recently? Um, no. Any uh contacted someone who's been sick recently? I don't think so. OK. I did just say sorry, I I did just get out of the hospital so maybe I could have had some contact. There's some exposure there. OK, so so when did you get out of the hospital? Uhm, two days ago. Two days ago, OK, and what were you in the hospital for? Uhm? I had, I broke my hip. OK, and did you have a surgery for your hip? No. They didn't think it was, surgery was needed. OK, so how long were you in the hospital for? About 4 days. Four days, OK. And in those four days what were they doing for you there? Just trying to help me just doing physiotherapy and helping me sort of get up and get out of bed. OK. And like giving me pain medications. And since, or like when you went home today, set up any kind of home care for you or anything like that? Yeah they did. A nurse comes in once a day. OK, and how about uh physiotherapy? Yeah, I don't know. They haven't really been coming in and I I asked the nurse and she said she's gonna look into it. Maybe something went wrong there. OK, Um, and after you've been discharged, have you been moving around at all, or if you've been pretty immobilized? I try, but it's been it's been hard like, specially the first couple of days. I don't think it did much. But since then, I've been, I've been trying to move around a little bit more. OK. Have you noticed any kind of swelling in your legs at all? Uh, no. No, nothing like that? No. Have you previously had any kind of clots, like a deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism? Anything like that? No. Have you had any previous diagnosis of cancer? I did in the past. About 10 years ago I had leukaemia. Leukaemia, OK. And are you still on any kind of treatment or do you see an oncologist or a mythologist? No, not anymore. Not anymore? OK. Um and so are you experiencing any kind of coughing up blood or anything like that? Uhm, no. No. OK. Um do you feel any pain in your caps at all? No. No, OK. Alright, other than that, are you having any symptoms of nausea and vomiting at all? Uhm, no I don't. OK, any fevers or chills? No. OK. Any night sweats or fatigue? No. Ok. Any diarrhea or constipation? No. None of those. OK. And besides the cancer, any previous medical conditions that you've been diagnosed with? No, I was pretty healthy before that. Ok, any history of any clotting or clotting disorders at all? No. OK. Do you take any medications currently? No, no, not anymore. OK, any allergies to any medications? Uhm, no. Ok. Um any family history of any lung or heart conditions? Um, no lung or heart conditions, no. OK. And currently right now do you work at all? Yeah I do. I um, I'm a banker. Banker OK. Uhm. And what's your living situation like? Where do you live with anyone or inward? Where do you live? I live at home with my husband and my two kids. Ok, and you smoke it all? No, I don't. OK. And do you use any recreational drugs like marijuana? Uh, sometimes I'll have marijuana with my friends, but I haven't really done that since the pandemic started. OK, and you drink alcohol? Yeah, I do come. I'll have maybe two or three glasses of beer on the weekend. OK. Alright, so these are all this questions I had for you today. We'll do a full physical examination next. Do you have any questions or concerns? No. OK, so yeah, right now one of the main things we want to rule out, especially since you had the sudden shortness of breath today after a history of just recent discharge and the hip fracture or injury, and you're immobilized we need to make sure you haven't developed a clot that has traveled to your lungs. So we'll do a full physical examination and get some blood work done initially and then see if you require any medications for if you need thrombolysis for to break up the clot. We'll, we might do some imaging as well, so yeah, well, we'll just start with that, and then we'll go from there. Sure. So you'll be in here for a few hours until we get all those things done and see if you need any admission at all to the hospital. Sure. Alright. Thank you. Welcome, take care.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Shortness of breath since this morning\n- Occurred during activities (walking to the kitchen)\n- Described as shallow, fast breaths\n- No previous episodes\n- Sharp chest pain with deep breaths\n- No cough, sore throat, chest pain, chills, fevers, dizziness, fainting, heart racing, nausea, vomiting, night sweats, fatigue, diarrhea, or constipation\n- No recent travel or contact with sick individuals\n- Recent hospital discharge (2 days ago) for hip fracture (no surgery, 4-day stay, physiotherapy, pain medications)\n- Home care nurse visits once a day, no physiotherapy at home yet\n- Limited mobility post-discharge, trying to move more recently\n- No leg swelling, pain in calves, or history of clots\n- PMHx: leukemia (10 years ago, no current treatment)\n- No other medical conditions or clotting disorders\n- No current medications or allergies\n- FHx: no long-term heart conditions\n- SocHx: banker, lives with husband and 2 kids, no smoking, occasional marijuana use, drinks 2-3 glasses of beer on weekends\n\nPast Medical History:\n- Hip fracture (recent, no surgery)\n- Leukemia (10 years ago, no current treatment)\n\nObjective:\n- Full physical examination planned\n- Blood work planned\n\nAssessment:\n- Rule out clot (possible pulmonary embolism)\n\nPlan:\n- Physical examination\n- Blood work\n- Possible imaging\n- Consider thrombolysis if clot confirmed\n- Monitor and determine need for hospital admission"}
{"ID": "soap_00010", "dialogue": "Are you married? Yes. Who do you have in your family? Me, my spouse and three happy kids. One of them is ready to go to college. Are you working? Yes and no. What does that mean? I was an attorney but retired from my official post two years back. I just couldn't stay back home and do nothing, so I just joined a private company and am working there part time. Sounds great. Do you smoke or drink? No. I am guessing no drugs either? Correct.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is married and has a spouse and three children.\n- One child is preparing to go to college.\n- Patient was an attorney but retired from their official position two years ago.\n- Currently working part-time at a private company.\n- Denies smoking, drinking alcohol, or using drugs.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment or clinical impressions provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00095", "dialogue": "Good morning, ma'am. Oh, good morning, doctor. Please, tell me about your living situation. Well, right now, I live at home with my kids and my dad. I see here that you're not able to go up the stairs well, can you tell me more about that? Yeah, I can't use the stairs without holding the rail the entire time. Holding the rail makes navigating the stairs easier? Yeah, but I still move very slow. I see. Do you smoke? Yeah, I smoke about a pack to a pack and a half a day. What about exercise? Do you workout? No, I don't regularly workout, I've never been good at it.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Difficulty using stairs\n- Severity: Slow movement, needs to hold the rail\n- Duration: Not specified\n- Associated symptoms: None explicitly mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: Smokes about a pack to a pack and a half a day\n- Family history: Lives with kids and dad (not directly relevant to medical history but mentioned)\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other concerns: None mentioned\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00089", "dialogue": "I was wondering if we could start with you telling us what brought you in? Sure, I've had this terrible headache for the past four days. Doesn't seem to be getting any better, and I feel hot like I have a fever. I've been coughing a lot so I am I'm worried I have COVID, so I'm here. OK. So I'll start with the headaches. When did when did that exactly started? Uh the headache started, OK so the coughing started four days ago. The headache started shortly after so I would say later on during that day and then the fever I've had for, fever I think I've had for the past two days. I haven't actually taken my temperature though. OK um. So, uh, the cough is started four days ago. Has the cough gotten better, worse, or stayed the same? The cough has stayed the same. OK, are you bringing up any phlegm or sputum with the cough? Um, it's not really phlegm, but sometimes I cough up, like bits of like. You know little bits that look like blood or something. OK, so you're seeing some like red when when you're coughing up? Mmmm OK, and how much of this are you seeing like, is it like a teaspoon amount or or more? Oh, it's it's very little. It's like maybe a little bit bigger than like the tip of a pencil. Oh OK, are you, OK, are you seeing this on like kind of tissues afterwards sort of thing? Yeah. OK, um and any triggers for the cough you can think of? No, it just seems to be there all day. Um, doesn't really get better with me trying to rest or not. OK, and is the cough there at night time too? Yes, it is definitely there at night. OK, um is it interfering with sleep? Ah yes, I haven't been sleeping well. OK, um, and with your cough have you had any difficulties with breathing? Um, no. Alright, and now, how about any wheezing? No wheezing. OK. And with this headache when it first came on, did you experience a like worst headache of your life, or a Thunder clap sensation? No, it's it's not that bad, but it just seems to be there all day long. OK, um have you had headaches like this before? No, I've never gotten headaches in the past. OK, uh is the headache associated with any nausea or vomiting? No. OK, and is the headache worse in the morning when you first wake up? No. OK, and does it have to get worse if you lie flat? No. OK, and has this headache been getting worse, staying the same, getting better? It's it's about the same. OK. Um and, um and you haven't checked the temperature yet, you said, but you felt hot? Yes, that's right. OK, any other symptoms that you've been experiencing? Not that I can think of. OK, there's just a few others that I wanted to ask about to be comprehensive. Have you had any eye pain or ear pain? No. How about any discharge from either your eyes or ears? No, nothing like that. Alright, have you had any changes to either your sense of smell or your sense of taste? No. Alright, and have you had a runny nose at all? Um no. And how about a sore throat? No. OK, and um, have you had any chills? Um no chills. How about night sweats? Um no nothing like that. I see, OK and over the last few months has your weight changed? Ah no, not that I noticed. OK, um have you been feeling tired or fatigued? No. OK, and have you had any chest pain? Um no. Alright, and how about any heart palpitations? None. Alright, in any episodes of feeling really dizzy or lightheaded. No. OK, and how about any fainting spells or feeling like you might faint? No, nothing like that. OK. And have you had any abdominal pain? Um no, no abdominal pain. Any diarrhea or constipation? No. How about any painful urination or feeling as though you need to urinate really frequently? Ah no nothing like that. OK, and have you had any skin changes like rashes or anything like that? No. OK, and how about any muscle aches or joint aches or just overall like body pains? Mmm no, not that I that not. Not that I've noticed, no. OK, is there anything in regards to your past health that you see a physician regularly for? Um No. Ah actually I I think at one point, um, I was worked up for something called familial hypercholesterolemia because it ran in the family. But I think those results were normal. OK, so do you, um, do you take a statin medication then? Yeah I take a low dose statin. OK, and when was your last lipid panel checked? I think it was two years ago. I see, and um do you remember if the LDL was normal at that time? Ah yes it was normal. OK, but since the, it was about two years ago, it might be worthwhile today just to also check a lipid panel just to be safe, but that'll be something we can talk about that more so at the end if that's OK. Sure, yeah, that would be great. OK, and any other medical conditions? No. OK. Have you had any surgeries before? C-section. How many children do you have? Ah just the one. OK, and were there any complications with the c-section? Um no no complications. OK, any reasons for hospitalizations? Nope. OK, any allergies to medications? No. Alright, any medications besides the statin that you take? Nope. OK, and in regards to your family history, have there been people who've had early heart attacks or strokes in the family? No. OK, but you do have familial hypercholesterolemia, do you have family members who have that? Yes OK so my Aunts had it, as well as my mother. OK um. And has there been any lung conditions that run in the family? Um, no lung conditions. How about any malignancy's or or cancers? Ah there was colon cancer on my dad side of the family, but I'm not too sure who had it. OK, have you heard of anybody having breast cancer? Breast cancer no. Alright, and how about ovarian cancer? No ovarian cancer. OK. And next I actually wanted to ask a few questions about your personal and lifestyle habits, but first, could you just tell me a little bit about your living situation right now? I live with my husband and my one son. OK, and sorry, how old is your son? He is 10. OK, um is anybody in the home been sick recently? No. OK, and how about is there anybody at work? At work no. No one's been sick. OK, have you traveled anywhere recently? We went up to we just went to um like a, we went on a hiking trail that was outside the city recently and there were a lot of people there and and we weren't wearing masks, but we, it seemed like we were keeping a good distance between each other so that that would be my only concern. OK for sure. Were people pretty close to each other on the paths when walking by on the hike? Ahh not like the whole time, but there would be times when you know people just kind of brushed by you to try to get ahead of you or something like that. I see OK. And do you smoke cigarettes? No. Um, do you smoke marijuana? Um no. OK, and do you drink alcohol? I don't. Do you use any other recreational drugs? Um no. OK, so that was all I wanted to ask about. Oh, actually one thing that I I did want to, I forgot to ask was, do you have any neck stiffness? No, not that I noticed. OK. So that was the last question that I wanted to ask about. Was there anything that you wanted to tell me that I I maybe forgot? No, I think you covered it all. I'm just wondering do you? Do you think it's COVID or something else? Well, it's definitely a possibility with the symptoms that you're having, since they can be so non- specific for covid it can be a lot of symptoms that overlap. So with having headache what sounds like you might have a fever and and this cough that sounds like it's pretty bad if you're bringing up some blood, it sounds like you might have irritated some of the bronchioles with the the coughing. So we will test for that and check a PCR nasal swab for COVID. I'm not, but we will also test for other viral illnesses and bacterial illnesses as well. It doesn't sound like you have meningitis right now just based on the symptoms where you don't have neck stiffness, but it'll be certainly something that we keep keep in mind while we are doing our investigations. OK great, thank you. Alright, you're welcome. We'll get those started. OK, sounds great.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 4-day history of cough (started 4 days ago, no change, present day and night, interfering with sleep, no triggers identified, no dyspnea or wheezing)\n- Coughing up small amounts of blood (slightly larger than pencil tip, seen on tissues)\n- Headache for the past 4 days (started shortly after cough, no change, not thunderclap, no prior history of similar headaches, no positional component, no nausea or vomiting)\n- Feeling hot for the past 2 days (no temperature taken)\n- Denies eye pain, ear pain, eye/ear discharge, changes in smell/taste, rhinorrhea, sore throat, chills, night sweats, weight changes, fatigue, chest pain, palpitations, dizziness, lightheadedness, syncope, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, dysuria, frequency, skin changes, myalgias, or arthralgias\n\nPMHx:\n- Worked up for familial hypercholesterolemia (results normal)\n- Takes low-dose statin (last lipid panel 2 years ago, LDL normal at that time)\n- C-section (1 child, no complications)\n- No hospitalizations\n- No allergies\n\nFHx:\n- Familial hypercholesterolemia (aunt, mother)\n- Colon cancer on paternal side (unsure of specific relative)\n- No lung conditions, breast cancer, or ovarian cancer\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives with husband and 10-year-old son\n- No known sick contacts at home or work\n- Recently went on a crowded hiking trail outside the city (no masks worn, attempted to maintain distance but occasional close contact with others on the path)\n- Non-smoker, non-drinker, no recreational drug use\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals not provided\n- No neck stiffness on exam\n\nAssessment:\n- Possible COVID-19 given presenting symptoms (cough, fever, headache)\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 PCR nasal swab\n- Testing for other viral and bacterial illnesses\n- Consider meningitis if symptoms evolve, though currently less likely given absence of neck stiffness\n- Check lipid panel given history of familial hypercholesterolemia and statin use with last panel 2 years ago"}
{"ID": "soap_00056", "dialogue": "Would you mind by starting with what brought you in today? Yeah, so I'm just coming in with my son. He's six years old and yeah, just over the last few days he's not been himself and he's been having this stomach ache. Uh, so it started around like I would say 3 days ago and then he's also having vomiting for the last two days, he's vomited in total six times over the last two days and then yesterday he also developed a fever as well. I managed to measure it and it was 38.3 degrees Celsius. OK, I see. So I just so that I understand the timeline, so he started to have abdominal pain about 3 days ago, started with having vomiting and diarrhea two days ago and you checked his temperature, he had a fever as of yesterday? Yeah. OK, and uh, was there anything that that he ate that was different over over these last few days? No, we've been strictly home food during like a pandemic like no, no real changes. OK, and what's his kind of behavior been like over these last couple of days? Yeah, so at first with the stomach pain he was a bit just irritable and just not himself, but especially yesterday with the fever just been very quiet and just kind of sleeping pretty much the whole day. OK, I see and uh, could you describe the vomit? Yes, so vomit like, it started two days ago. The first day it was just like he puked up the things that he had eaten, but yesterday it was it was mainly just like uh just yellowish material, I couldn't really tell if it was food or not. OK, have you noticed any bile or blood in the vomit? Uh no I didn't notice any blood and I didn't notice any green material. It was mainly just like like normal vomit, like yellowish color. OK, I see and, has the what was the vomiting triggered by anything that you're aware of, like eating or drinking? Uhm? Yeah, two days ago it was it was after after lunch when he first vomited. But after that it's just been kind of random. I see OK, and, uh, and with regards to his diarrhea, has there been any any blood or or any color changes? No, no, I didn't notice any blood at all. It's definitely just very very watery and he's had to go probably even just in the last day, probably around like six or seven times. OK, um, and he's vomited how many times? So in total I can count 6 times. OK, and, has he been complaining of any headaches at all? No, not that I know. No. OK, and how about any ear pain or or eye pain? No, not that he's mentioned to me. OK, and any eye redness? Nope. OK, and as he had a runny nose? No, not really. OK and has he had a sore throat or any pain with swallowing? No. OK, have you had any loss of taste or loss of smell? Uh no, not that he has mentioned to me now. OK, and has he had a cough? Uh, no, not really. OK, and how about any shortness of breath or wheezing? No. OK, and has he had any any pain anywhere? Any chest pain? Um, he did, he did point to his stomach like it seems like he was having with all the vomiting like, he was having some stomach pain as well. OK, is there any specific location that that he's feeling the pain? Well, it's hard to say. He was just pointing at the center of his belly like, I don't know if it's like, I tried pressing on it and he didn't feel any pain then, but I think it's just kind of all over his stomach. OK, and has he had any urinary problems like painful voiding or been peeing more often? No, that's been OK. Have you noticed any rashes or skin changes? Uh, no, not not really, no. OK. And has he had any muscle aches or uh, or joint pains that that he's mentioned? No, no he hasn't mentioned anything like that. OK, and he's had a fever, has he had the chills? Yeah, so that's that's what I, that prompted me to take his temperature yesterday around the afternoon, like we had to wrap him up with like more than two blankets and he was still feeling cold so, yeah, definitely was having chills. OK, and and has there been any concerns with his growth, either weight or or height? So he's been going along the charts pretty well, like no concerns during his growth. Otherwise, he's a very healthy child. OK, so no medical diagnosis previously? No nothing like that. OK, and uh, yeah, is he meeting all developmental milestones OK? Like gross motor, fine motor, doing well at school? Yeah yeah, he didn't have any issues with walking, running, he's pretty social with all his peers. He's doing well at school, no complaints. OK, excellent and you know, no medical conditions, but has there been any reasons for being in the hospital before? No, not at all. We, yeah, we haven't really gone to the doctor with him after like all those vaccinations that he had to do, so he's been good. OK, great, so immunizations are up to date? Yeah, immunizations all up to date. Excellent and does he have any allergies? Uh, no, uh, not not anything that we know of yet. OK, and I just wanted to ask about his birth history. Were there any complications throughout throughout pregnancy, either mom being sick or having any any issues herself? No, so it was a normal vaginal delivery, uh no issues, uh he was born at 39 weeks, didn't need to stay in the hospital at all. Yeah, it was a pretty smooth pregnancy compared to some of the other ones. OK, oh, that's that's great. Do you remember the Apgar score or his birth weight? Uhm, I don't exactly remember his Apgar scores, I think they were like just just they they were normal like there was nothing concerning about them, and birth weight I think he was probably around 3 kilograms. OK, great so, yeah healthy birth weight at term it sounds like. And so, healthy, does he take any medications regularly? No, no medications. OK, and could you tell me about who is living with currently and then who's looking after him? Yeah, so he's living with myself, his mother and, and then my husband, and he has two older siblings. One is a boy, seven years old, and another one like a 10 year old girl. OK, and has anybody in the house been sick? Uh no, no one has been sick recently, however he does, they did let them go back into school at one point, so I don't know, I don't know, so that was just last week, so I don't know if he caught something from school. OK, but nobody that you know that's had similar symptoms? No. OK, and have you traveled anywhere? No, we've been, we've been at home. OK, uh, and then you know recent changes to anything in the diet you've mentioned? No, that's been fine. OK, and any concerns for safety in the home, whether physical or or verbal abuse? No, no concerns like that. OK, yeah a question that we have to ask everybody and is there any smoking in the home? No, no, we don't smoke. OK, great then, are there any pets? Nope, no pets. Alright, and uh and in the family, has there been anybody who's had any heart problems or any lung problems? Uh, we are getting the 10 year old tested for just uh, asthma. OK. But nothing diagnosed yet, no. OK, anybody in the family have any congenital diseases or any genetic diseases? No, not that I know of. OK, uh, anybody in the family have celiac disease or Crohn's disease? No, no, uh no history of any kind of bowel diseases. OK, so I think that's, uh, everything I want I wanted to ask about today. Uh, was there anything that maybe I I forgot to ask or or that I missed that you wanted to tell me? Uh, yeah, no it's, it's also just like I was just reading up like, can it be like I don't know if it can be COVID as well, if it can, it can do this as well? It is possible. Uh, it's uh, especially with nonspecific symptoms, COVID can cause a variety of different presentations, and there for some people it can be more gastrointestinal issues like like your son has with vomiting and diarrhea and belly pain. But it also could be well, uh, another type of gastrointestinal virus itself or or a viral illness like the common cold, which can also end up causing symptoms like this as well. So we will, I will go ahead and do a COVID test today, along with some other blood work. And maybe increase the hydration as well, because it sounds like he has lost a lot of fluid over these last couple of days. So we will check electrolytes and go from there on the test results. Alright, sounds good. Thank you so much.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 6 yo M presents with abdominal pain x 3 days, vomiting x 2 days (6 episodes total), and fever (38.3\u00b0C) x 1 day\n- Vomitus: Initially food contents, now yellowish material without blood or bile\n- Diarrhea x 1 day (6-7 episodes), watery without blood\n- Fever associated with chills requiring multiple blankets\n- Abdominal pain generalized, non-tender to palpation\n- Denies headache, ear/eye pain, eye redness, rhinorrhea, sore throat, dysphagia, anosmia, ageusia, cough, dyspnea, wheezing, chest pain, dysuria, increased urinary frequency, rash, myalgia, arthralgia\n- Behavior: Irritable initially, now lethargic\n- No sick contacts, recent travel, or diet changes\n- Attends school (possible infectious exposure)\n\nPMHx:\n- Term infant, SVD, no complications\n- Up-to-date on immunizations\n- No prior hospitalizations or surgeries\n- No known allergies\n- No medications\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives with mother, father, brother (7 yo), sister (10 yo)\n- No smokers in the home\n- No pets\n- No safety concerns\n\nFHx:\n- Sister undergoing evaluation for asthma\n- No congenital/genetic disorders\n- No IBD or celiac disease\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals not provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Acute gastroenteritis (viral vs. COVID-19)\n- Dehydration\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 testing\n- CBC, CMP\n- IV fluids for rehydration\n- Anticipate discharge with supportive care once hydrated and tolerating PO"}
{"ID": "soap_00021", "dialogue": "Any past medical history like any diabetes or high blood pressure or any heart disease? No, I don't remember any such thing. How about any surgeries in the past? No. No surgeries at all.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient does not recall any past medical history of diabetes, high blood pressure, or heart disease.\n- Patient has not had any surgeries in the past.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00041", "dialogue": "You can continue working, I just want you to be careful. Thanks Doctor. I will be. Great.", "note": "Subjective: \n- Patient expresses gratitude to the doctor. \n- Patient intends to continue working but is advised to be careful.\n\nObjective: \n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment: \n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.)\n\nPlan: \n- Patient is advised to be careful while continuing to work."}
{"ID": "soap_00066", "dialogue": "I will do a quick exam on you. What kind of exam? I'm just checking for lymph nodes. Whenever there is an infection those guys are the first ones to be noticed with swelling. Okay, so let's first check your neck area. It looks good. I don't feel any inflamed lymph node. Your armpits look good too and the last place to check is your groin area. It is all good. No sign of infection.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient mentioned concern about potential infection.\n- No specific symptoms, severity, or duration of symptoms provided.\n- No relevant medical history, family history, or allergies mentioned.\n- Patient appears to be concerned about lymph node swelling.\n\nObjective:\n- Physical examination findings:\n - Neck area: No inflamed lymph nodes detected.\n - Armpits: No abnormalities noted.\n - Groin area: No signs of infection.\n\nAssessment:\n- No specific diagnoses or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan or follow-up actions explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00076", "dialogue": "Hi there, what's going on? Guest_family: I am her daughter. I brought her to the emergency. She lives in a skilled nursing home. Today, when I went to meet her, I found her with the right side of her face drooping. I immediately rushed her here. Oh, you did the right thing. Ma'am, how are you feeling? Guest_family: She has dementia, most of the time she doesn't even remember me. Oh, okay.How old is she? Guest_family: She is eighty three. She also has a history of stroke after which she developed this condition where she knows what she wants to speak but is unable to make the word or sentence. Oh, I see. She has expressive aphasia, correct? Guest_family: Yes, that. Okay, let me check her. She does have a droop to her right side of the face. Ma'am, do you feel any weakness on the right side of your body? Guest_family: She says yes. She had her C T scan earlier today and I have the report. It does not show any acute attack or anything. Guest_family: Okay! But it does show that she has a new onset of a stroke. Guest_family: Is there anything to worry? I think we need to admit her for observation. We need to monitor her. We will also get her a proper treatment for her urinary infection which is not getting better due to resistance to the oral medicines. Guest_family: Yeah, sounds good! Yeah, let me talk to the nurse. Guest_family: Thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is an 83-year-old female with dementia.\n- Right side of face drooping observed by daughter.\n- History of stroke leading to expressive aphasia (difficulty in speaking).\n- Patient reports feeling weakness on the right side of her body.\n- Daughter expresses concern about the patient's condition and asks if there is anything to worry about.\n\nObjective:\n- Right side of the face droop noted on examination.\n- CT scan performed earlier today shows no acute attack, but indicates a new onset of stroke.\n- Patient has a urinary infection that is not improving due to resistance to oral medications.\n\nAssessment:\n- New onset of stroke.\n- Expressive aphasia.\n- Urinary infection with resistance to oral medications.\n\nPlan:\n- Admit the patient for observation.\n- Monitor the patient's condition.\n- Provide proper treatment for the urinary infection."}
{"ID": "soap_00075", "dialogue": "What brought you in today? Yeah, so uh, just randomly like 2 days ago I just started having this chest pain when I'm breathing and it's just been getting worse and then because of that, I'm finding that I'm very short of breath as well, so I just got really concerned. OK, I see. When did the chest pain start? So, there was some discomfort like just 2 days ago, but then just yesterday it started getting very painful when I was breathing. So yeah, I would say 1 day ago. OK, and were you doing anything at the time when the chest pain started? Like did you get in a car accident maybe? Or any trauma to the chest or anything you can think of? No, not really. I was just at home and then I noticed that when, I like, take a deep breath in, it's just, like, a very sharp pain. It's kind of like in my right side of the chest and it feels like a stabbing pain. OK, and so the pain comes on when you breathe in, or yeah, when you take a deep breath. Does the pain come on at any other points or does it go away? Uh, it's mostly, yeah, I feel the sharp pain when I breathe in. OK, so the pain is intermittent then, it sounds like. And has there been anything you've tried that's made the pain better? Not really, just more shallow breathing, and it hurts less with that, but that's about it. OK, and are there any positions that help make the pain any better like leaning forward or laying down? Not really, it's uh, it's pretty much the same, I think. OK, and uh, you mentioned you had shortness of breath as well. When does this come on? Since yesterday it's been pretty constant and it's more like, Uh, just uh, I'm really having trouble getting any deep breaths in, so I feel like I'm having to just breathe faster and more shallow. I see, OK, and has anything like this happened to you before? No, never. OK, and have you been experiencing any headaches? No. How about any viral symptoms, like eye redness, nasal discharge, or nasal congestion or cough? I have been having a little bit of a cough, but that's it, like, it kind of started today, but it's nothing too significant. OK, alright, is the cough dry or have you brought up sputum? It is dry. OK, and have you had a sore throat? Uh, no. How about a loss of taste or a loss of smell? No. OK, how has your appetite been? My appetite's been pretty good. It's like, definitely with the pain, I don't really feel like eating today, but I don't think it's changed. OK, so has your weight been stable over the last several months. Yeah, weight has been stable. OK, and have you had any fevers, chills, or night sweats? Uhm, Nope. OK, and have you had any episodes where you feel like your heart is, like, racing or beating out of your chest? No, I don't think so. Any dizziness or Lightheadedness? No. OK, and have you had any wheezing? No, I don't think so. OK, and with the cough, have you brought up any blood? No. OK, and have you had any, Uh, like urinary problems? No. Any bowel problems like diarrhea or Constipation? No, nothing like that. And any abdominal pain? No. Have you had any nausea or vomiting? No. OK, and have you had any muscle aches, joint pains or skin changes? No. OK, and uhm, in the past have you been diagnosed with any medical conditions? No, I've been pretty healthy. OK, so do you take any medications regularly? No, nothing at all. And do you have any allergies? No, no allergies. Any hospitalizations in the past or any surgeries? No. OK, and currently, can you tell me just a little bit about your living situation? Yeah, so I just live with uh, a couple of my roommates. I'm going to University. OK. I live in like a student housing situation. OK, has anybody in the house been sick or anybody that you've been around been sick? No, we've been all pretty healthy. OK, and do you smoke cigarettes or use any like vape products or anything like that? No, I haven't ever smoked. I do smoke weed like, I would say, once or twice a week. OK, and how much would you say? Like 1 gram or less or? 1 gram probably lasts like 2 weeks. OK, and do you drink alcohol? Yeah, just a couple of beers maybe on the weekend. OK, and are you currently sexually active? Yeah, just one partner, my girlfriend. OK, and, do you use condoms? Yeah, we use condoms, we're pretty safe. OK, and have you had a sexually transmitted infection before? No, never. OK, any concerns right now or anything like that? No, uh, we've been pretty safe and we've both been tested before and everything, so I'm not really concerned. OK, great and has anybody in the family had like a similar presentation before, like any lung problems or heart problems? No, I think my sister, she has asthma, but that's about it. OK, and uh, any cancers that run in the family? Not that I know of. OK, so it sounds like you had this uncomfortable feeling in your chest about two days ago, but the pain really came on yesterday and it's really painful when you have a deep deep breath. So, in conjunction with your symptoms of not being able to take a deep breath and being short of breath, it sounds like you possibly have what's called a pneumothorax and so we'll do a physical exam and do a chest X-ray as well to examine this and check vitals as well to make sure that you're work of breathing is OK and we can do some blood work and go from there. OK, yeah, sounds good. Should be like, worried at all? I think right now, you're speaking in full sentences and you're not short of breath at rest, typically, when this this happens, if it's small enough, it'll usually resolve on its own and so we'll get some more information from the imaging and the lab results, but it's reassuring that you haven't been getting worse since it happened. OK, alright, sounds good. Thank you so much.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Chest pain when breathing, started 2 days ago, worsened yesterday\n- Sharp, stabbing pain on the right side of the chest, intermittent, worse with deep breaths\n- Shortness of breath since yesterday, constant, difficulty taking deep breaths, shallow and faster breathing\n- Dry cough started today, not significant\n- No headaches, viral symptoms (eye redness, nasal discharge, nasal congestion), sore throat, loss of taste or smell\n- Appetite good, but decreased today due to pain\n- Weight stable\n- No fevers, chills, night sweats, palpitations, dizziness, lightheadedness, wheezing, hemoptysis, urinary problems, bowel problems, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, muscle aches, joint pains, or skin changes\n\nPast Medical History:\n- No known medical conditions\n- No medications\n- No allergies\n- No hospitalisations or surgeries\n\nSocial History:\n- Lives with roommates in student housing, attending university\n- No recent illnesses among housemates\n- No smoking, occasional marijuana use (1 gram lasts 2 weeks)\n- Occasional alcohol consumption (a couple of beers on weekends)\n- Sexually active with one partner, uses condoms, no history of STIs\n\nFamily History:\n- Sister has asthma\n- No known family history of lung or heart problems, or cancers\n\nObjective:\n- Physical exam and vitals to be performed\n- Chest X-ray planned\n- Blood work planned\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely diagnosis: Pneumothorax\n\nPlan:\n- Physical exam\n- Chest X-ray\n- Blood work\n- Monitor symptoms and follow-up based on imaging and lab results"}
{"ID": "soap_00034", "dialogue": "What medications are you currently taking? I am a diabetic, so I take Insulin. How much Insulin do you take? It varies from day to day.", "note": "Subjective: \n- Patient is diabetic. \n- Currently taking insulin. \n- Insulin dosage varies from day to day. \n\nObjective: \n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment: \n- [No clinician's interpretation or diagnosis provided.]\n\nPlan: \n- [No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00090", "dialogue": "How may I help you? I just came in here today, I've been having a lot of shoulder pain that's just kind of left me not doing any of my activities recently. Just in my left shoulder. OK and when, when did this pain start? It started 5 days ago but it became pretty bad and I've been kind of having like a somewhat of a pain for the last couple weeks, but it got really bad just this week. OK, did you have any trauma to the shoulder like any any injuries or triggers that could have caused the pain? Uhm I have been just going to the gym more frequently over the last few months and lifting weights and things like that. But just this week I wasn't even really able to do that either. OK, when you were lifting weights like a few days ago like was there an episode where there was an acute onset of the pain, where it got worse kind of like right away. I think it was more progressive, but five days ago I do feel that there was like just. I was having some pain before that, but then I just noticed while working on I I started having more pain. But yeah I don't think there was anything like sudden that happened though. Oh OK, but it yeah it was progressing and then it got acutely worse with the weight lifting. It sounds like. Maybe like I did increase my weight like in the last two weeks. So I did feel like a little bit more pain with the increase last week. OK, I see an in the left shoulder do you feel the pain at the front at the sides or or back? It hurts, particularly like in the front part of the shoulder. OK, do you feel that it's radiating anywhere else? Just my whole left shoulder, nothing else really. OK. And could you describe the character or the quality of the pain? Is it sharp, or is it a key? It's more achy. OK. But but certain like some movements so it can get like kind of sharp. OK, and has there been anything that has made the pain worse? Uhm, if I just if I carry anything with my left arm or if I have to lift anything or place it on my shoulder like it really hurts. OK, and have you done anything that's made it better? I just like resting it, not carrying anything. And trying not to lie on my shoulder. OK, so you're sleeping on the others on the right side then. Yeah. OK alright, how would what would you say the severity of the pain is on a scale of 1 to 10. Uhm, if I'm not doing it, doing anything or not doing much, it's probably like a two or three, but if I try to carry anything it goes up to like probably like a 7. OK, uh, and um. Do you do any repetitive kind of like overhead activities? Things like playing basketball or just lifting things like above your head at the gym? Um not recently just just like normal weight exercise, maybe with the bands I do some overhead exercises, but in terms of sports I I play or I used to play a lot of tennis. I did have some issues with the same shoulder, which is why I kind of stopped playing tennis. My doctor said it, I might have overused that shoulder, but that's that was like a couple of years back so I thought it must have healed by now. OK, so previous overuse injury of the left shoulder? Mmmm OK, and uh. I'm have you had any trauma to the shoulder? Um no not that I know of. OK, have you had any fevers or chills? No. Have you had any night sweats? No. OK, have you had any skin rashes or or skin changes? No. OK so even over the area you haven't noticed any redness or like soft tissue swelling? No. OK. Have you had any chest pain? Nope. OK, how about any shortness of breath? No, not that I've noticed. OK, have you had pains and any other joints? No, just my shoulder. OK And. Um, Have you had any recent infections or or symptoms such as like a runny nose or sore throat or even a cough? No, nothing like that. OK, and how about any nausea or vomiting? Um nope. OK. And um. How has your sleep been these past couple of nights? So you mentioned you haven't been able to lay on your left side? Yeah, it's been pretty painful like I kind of wake up every time I accidentally go on to my left side. OK, so you're getting worse thing a pain at night? Yup. OK. Have you had any weakness? Do you feel like you have less strength in your arm? Um I do you feel that there might be some less strength in my left arm, but it's I'm not really using it much so it's hard to tell. OK, And how about a range of motion. Do you feel as though you could lift your left arm up to like, scratch your head and back? It does hurt like when I try to lift my arm up. OK, so it sounds like a range of motion is decreased? Mmm OK, and have you had any altered sensation of your arms being like numbness or tingling? No, I haven't felt any of that. OK. Um and have you had any changes to your bowel habits like constipation or diarrhea? Nope, nothing like that. Have you had any abdominal pain? Nope. And have you had any urinary problems? No. OK, and in the past have you been diagnosed with any medical conditions? Uh. No, I've been a pretty healthy actually just just that overuse injury like two years ago. OK, great and do you have any allergies to medications? No. OK, do you take any medications regularly? Just recently I've been taking a lot more of the Advil, but that's about it. OK, I and have you had any hospitalizations or prior surgeries? No, no prior surgeries or hospitalizations. OK, and could you tell me a little bit about your living situation currently, like who you're living with, and whereabouts? Yeah, so I'm living with with my wife in a small condominium in downtown. OK, and are you working currently? Yeah, I'm working as a real estate agent. OK, uh, and do you drink alcohol? Rarely just on some occasions, but not regularly. No. OK. How about smoking cigarettes or cannabis? No, I've never smoked. Do you use any recreational drugs or have you in the past? Nope OK. Um. And so so the next part, just for the physical exam. So do you have pain with shoulder AB duction. Uhm yes. So just to yeah, I do have some pain. Especially from. Yeah yeah pain throughout AB duction. OK, is there any like pain that's like parts of that that range of motion that are more painful for you? yeah, so probably from 60 degrees to 120 degrees is more painful. OK. And. If your arm is outstretched and your thumb pointing down so your arm is internally rotated does that give you pain? Yeah, if yeah, not not just like that, but if you were to like press down on it it the internal rotation does cause a little bit of pain. OK. OK, so it sounds like a positive jobes test and a positive Neer's test as well as a painful arc for your shoulder. And is there pain to palpation? Uh. Just to, yeah, definitely just at the front of the shoulder there is some some pain over with palpation. OK, that sensation is normal, reflexes are normal and strength is slightly reduced as well. OK, so that was everything that I wanted to ask on history and check on the physical exam as well. Was there anything else that you wanted to mention today that I maybe forgot? No, I think that's it. OK, um so based onI know that the symptoms today along with your age and prior history of an overuse injury of the left shoulder, it certainly raises concern of having a rotator cuff tear or um a rotator cuff impingement syndrome and where there is inflammation there not necessarily actually a full tear of the muscle that's causing the pain. So, uh, those would be the two most likely things. Yeah, their rotator cuff tear or subacromial impingement syndrome. And so we were going to get an ultrasound of the left shoulder to evaluate the structures and possibly some other imaging such as an X Ray or MRI to determine the extent of the injury in the left shoulder. OK, sounds good. Thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Left shoulder pain for the last couple weeks, worsened over the past 5 days\n- Pain is achy in character, with some sharp pain on certain movements (carrying objects, lifting arm overhead)\n- Pain is worse with activity (lifting weights at gym, carrying objects) and lying on left side; better with rest and avoiding use of left arm\n- Severity: 2-3/10 at rest, 7/10 with activity\n- No recent trauma, but h/o overuse injury to left shoulder 2 years ago due to tennis\n- Denies fevers, chills, night sweats, skin changes, chest pain, SOB, other joint pains, recent infections, N/V, abd pain, bowel/bladder changes \n- Reports decreased strength in left arm and reduced ROM due to pain\n- Difficulty sleeping due to pain when lying on left side\n\nPMHx:\n- Left shoulder overuse injury 2 years ago related to tennis \n- Otherwise healthy\n\nMedications: \n- Advil PRN recently for shoulder pain\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives with wife in condo\n- Works as real estate agent\n- Rare alcohol use, denies smoking or recreational drug use\n\nObjective:\n- Pain with shoulder abduction, especially 60-120 degrees (painful arc)\n- Positive Job's test and Neer's test \n- Tenderness to palpation over anterior shoulder\n- Slightly reduced strength in left arm\n- Sensation and reflexes normal\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely rotator cuff tear or subacromial impingement syndrome\n\nPlan:\n- Left shoulder ultrasound to evaluate structures \n- Consider X-ray or MRI to determine extent of injury"}
{"ID": "soap_00007", "dialogue": "What brought you in today? Sure, I'm I'm just having a lot of chest pain and and so I thought I should get it checked out. OK, before we start, could you remind me of your gender and age? Sure 39, I'm a male. OK, and so when did this chest pain start? It started last night, but it's becoming sharper. OK, and where is this pain located? It's located on the left side of my chest. OK, and, so how long has it been going on for then if it started last night? So I guess it would be a couple of hours now, maybe like 8. OK. Has it been constant throughout that time, or uh, or changing? I would say it's been pretty constant, yeah. OK, and how would you describe the pain? People will use words sometimes like sharp, burning, achy. I'd say it's pretty sharp, yeah. Sharp OK. Uh, anything that you have done tried since last night that's made the pain better? Um not laying down helps. OK, so do you find laying down makes the pain worse? Yes, definitely. OK, do you find that the pain is radiating anywhere? No. OK, and is there anything else that makes the pain worse besides laying down? Not that I've noticed, no. OK, so not like taking a deep breath or anything like that? Maybe taking a deep breath. Yeah. OK. And when the pain started, could you tell me uh, could you think of anything that you were doing at the time? I mean, I was moving some furniture around, but, that I've done that before. OK, so you didn't feel like you hurt yourself when you were doing that? No. OK, and in regards to how severe the pain is on a scale of 1 to 10, 10 being the worst pain you've ever felt, how severe would you say the pain is? I'd say it's like a seven or eight. It's pretty bad. OK, and with the pain, do you have any other associated symptoms? I feel a little lightheaded and I'm having some trouble breathing. OK. Have you had any loss of consciousness? No. OK. Uh, have you been experiencing any like racing of the heart? Um, a little bit, yeah. OK. And have you been sweaty at all? Just from the from having issues breathing. OK, have you been having issues breathing since the pain started? Yes. OK. Um recently have you had any periods of time where you like have been immobilized or or, you haven't been like able to move around a lot? No no. OK. And have you been feeling sick at all? Any infectious symptoms? No. OK, have you had any nausea or vomiting? No. Any fevers or chills? No. OK, how about any abdominal pain? No. Any urinary problems? No. Or bowel problems? No. OK, have you had a cough? No. OK. You haven't brought up any blood? No. OK, have you had a wheeze with your difficulty breathing? No, not that I've heard. OK, any changes to the breath sounds at all like any noisy breathing? No. Well, I guess if when I'm really having trouble breathing, yeah. OK. Has anything like this ever happened to you before? No. No, OK. And have you had any night sweats? No. Alright, and then how about any rashes or skin changes? No rashes, but I guess like my neck seems to be a little swollen. OK, do you have any neck pain? No. OK, have you had any like accidents like a car accident or anything where you really jerked your neck? No. OK. Um any any trauma at all to the chest or or back? No. OK, so just in regards to past medical history, do you have any prior medical conditions? No. OK, any recent hospitalizations? No. OK, any prior surgeries? No. OK, do you take any medications regularly? Are they prescribed or over the counter? No. Alright, how about any allergies to medications? None. Alright, any immunizations or are they up to date? They are all up to date. Excellent. Alright, and could you tell me a little bit about your living situation currently? Sure, I live in an apartment by myself. I, uh, yep, that's about it. OK, and how do you support yourself financially? I'm an accountant. OK, sounds like a pretty stressful job or that it can be. Do you smoke cigarettes? I do. OK, and how much do you smoke? I smoke about a pack a day. OK, how long have you been smoking for? For the past 10 to 15 years. OK, and do you smoke cannabis? Uh sometimes. Uh, how much marijuana would you smoke per per week? Per week, maybe about 5 milligrams. Not that much. OK, and do you use any other recreational drugs like cocaine, crystal, meth, opioids? No. OK. Have you used IV drugs before? No. OK. And do you drink alcohol? I do. OK. How much alcohol do you drink each week? Uhm about I would say I have like one or two drinks a day, so about 10 drinks a week. OK, uh, yeah and um alright, and then briefly, could you tell me a little bit about your like diet and exercise? Sure, I try to eat healthy for dinner at least, but most of my lunches are, uh I eat out. And then in terms of exercise, I try to exercise every other day, I run for about half an hour. No. OK, has anybody in the family had a heart attack before? Actually, yes, my father had a heart attack when he was 45. OK, and anybody in the family have cholesterol problems? I think my father did. I see OK, and how about anybody in the family have a stroke? No strokes. OK, and then any cancers in the family? No. OK, and is there anything else that you wanted to tell me about today that that I on on history? No, I don't think so. I think you asked me everything.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Chest pain\n- Duration: Started last night, constant for ~8 hours\n- Location: Left side of chest\n- Quality: Sharp\n- Severity: 7-8/10\n- Worsens: Laying down, deep breaths\n- Alleviates: Not laying down\n- Associated symptoms: Lightheadedness, trouble breathing, racing heart, sweating, noisy breathing, neck swelling\n- No radiation of pain\n- No previous episodes\n\nPast Medical History:\n- No prior medical conditions\n- No recent hospitalisations\n- No prior surgeries\n- Medications: None\n- Allergies: None\n- Immunisations: Up to date\n- SocHx: Lives alone in an apartment, accountant, smokes 1 pack/day for 10-15 years, occasional cannabis use (~5mg/week), drinks 1-2 drinks/day (~10 drinks/week), tries to eat healthy dinners, eats out for lunch, exercises every other day (runs for 30 mins)\n- FHx: Father had a heart attack at 45, father had cholesterol problems, no strokes, no cancers\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals: Not provided\n- Physical exam: Not provided\n- Investigations with results: Not provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Not provided\n\nPlan:\n- Not provided"}
{"ID": "soap_00078", "dialogue": "and lasts for only a few minutes in duration. It resolves when he covers one eye. It is worse when looking at distant objects and objects off to either side of midline. There are no other symptoms associated with the diplopia.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Diplopia (double vision)\n- Severity: Not explicitly stated\n- Duration: Lasts for only a few minutes\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: None mentioned\n- Family history: None mentioned\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other concerns: Diplopia resolves when covering one eye; worse when looking at distant objects and objects off to either side of midline.\n\nObjective:\n- No objective data mentioned.\n\nAssessment:\n- No assessment or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No plan mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00043", "dialogue": "How is your family doing? They all are good. Are you aware of any medical issues in your family? No, not to my knowledge.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient reports that their family is doing well.\n- No known medical issues in the family.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided)"}
{"ID": "soap_00000", "dialogue": "Good afternoon, champ, how you holding up? Good afternoon, Doctor, I have a lot of lower back pain. Oh no, before we begin, how old are you, sir and which hand do you write with? I'm seventy five now. Right. Great, so tell me, how long have you had this lower back pain? It's been about ten days now. Have your symptoms improved at all since they began? No, they keep getting worse. Does the pain radiate into your legs? Yes, it started radiating down my right leg three days after the lower back pain began, and then the left leg three days after the right. The next day I could barely walk, the pain was so severe. Do you remember the initial date of the beginning of your low back pain? Um, it was on December third nineteen ninety five. Have you seen another doctor for this pain? Yes, I saw my local physician, um, it was on December eleventh, but he wasn't able to pinpoint what was going on. What kind of treatments did he recommend? He gave me some antiinflammatories and sent me on my way. Did you drive yourself? Yes, I did. How did the antiinflammatories work? Um, by the time I got home, I could hardly walk due to the back pain, and I had weakness in my legs. I was, however, able to feed my dogs, and I fixed myself some dinner. Was there any leg numbness? Yes. Since December eleventh. Have you seen an orthopedist for this pain? Yes, I saw one the day after my primary care physician, but my legs gave out and I fell while walking to my car because of the leg weakness and back pain. What kind of imaging studies did that orthopedist do? He did a C T and x rays of my lower back, but he didn't see anything wrong. He recommended I follow up at U I H C. The imaging was on my L S Spine.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Lower back pain, radiating pain down the right leg, then the left leg.\n- Severity: Severe pain, described as \"I could barely walk\" and \"the pain was so severe.\"\n- Duration: Lower back pain for about ten days; radiating pain started three days after back pain began (right leg), then left leg three days later.\n- Associated symptoms: Weakness in the legs, leg numbness since December eleventh.\n- Relevant medical history: Saw local physician on December eleventh; received anti-inflammatory medications which were ineffective.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: Patient expressed difficulty walking and concerns about the severity of their symptoms.\n\nObjective:\n- Physical examination findings: Not provided.\n- Diagnostic test results: CT and X-rays of the lower back performed by the orthopedist, but no abnormalities were noted.\n- Vital signs: Not provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Diagnoses: Not explicitly stated, but indicated severe lower back pain with radiculopathy.\n- Differential diagnoses: Not provided.\n- Clinical impressions: Not explicitly stated.\n\nPlan:\n- Follow up at U I H C as recommended by the orthopedist. \n- No specific medications, treatments, or further tests mentioned in the conversation."}
{"ID": "soap_00055", "dialogue": "How's it going? I haven't seen you in a long time. Hey, Doctor. My headaches have been getting worse. Remind me again. Does you family have a history of migraines. I don't think so. Okay. Let's perform some tests.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Headaches\n- Severity: Getting worse\n- Duration: Not specified\n- Associated symptoms: Not mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: Family history of migraines (denied by patient)\n- Family history: No known history of migraines\n- Allergies: Not mentioned\n- Other concerns: None explicitly mentioned\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation provided)\n\nPlan:\n- Perform some tests"}
{"ID": "soap_00035", "dialogue": "Do you have any past or present medical conditions? No.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No past or present medical conditions reported by the patient.\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00062", "dialogue": "Good morning, doctor. So, I've reviewed your past notes, are there any other diagnoses I should know about? Well, I've had depression for a while now. Thank you for sharing that with me.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient has a history of depression for a while.\n- Patient inquired about other potential diagnoses.\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00032", "dialogue": "Is this baby number two? Yes, this is baby number two. How many times have you been pregnant, including miscarriages or abortions if any? I've been pregnant two times in total. No miscarriages or abortions. When was your last period? About a month ago. And how would you describe your periods? Light, normal, heavy? Pretty normal. Any abnormal pap smears? No, fortunately they've all been normal. That's wonderful to hear. And my last question for you is if you've ever gotten a mammogram? I haven't gotten one before. Do I need to get one? I just turned thirty nine. What's the recommended age again? It depends. For example, the recommended age would be younger for someone who has a family history of breast cancer. Given that you don't have a positive family history of breast cancer, I'd advise getting one in a few years at the age of forty five.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is pregnant with baby number two.\n- Total pregnancies: 2 (no miscarriages or abortions).\n- Last menstrual period: about a month ago.\n- Menstrual cycle: described as pretty normal.\n- No history of abnormal pap smears.\n- Patient has never had a mammogram.\n- Patient is concerned about the necessity of a mammogram at age 39 and inquires about the recommended age.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No diagnoses or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Advise the patient to get a mammogram in a few years, at age 45, due to lack of positive family history of breast cancer."}
{"ID": "soap_00059", "dialogue": "Besides pain in your left eye, are you having any other problems? No. Do you happen to have a history of eye problems? None.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Pain in the left eye\n- Severity: Not specified\n- Duration: Not specified\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned\n- Relevant medical history: None mentioned\n- Family history: None mentioned\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other concerns: None mentioned\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided]"}
{"ID": "soap_00048", "dialogue": "Tell me about your family medical history. No one in my family has any health problems.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Family medical history: No one in the family has any health problems.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided]"}
{"ID": "soap_00049", "dialogue": "What types of surgeries have you had in the past? I had gall bladder and appendix surgery then they also took out my ovaries. Okay.", "note": "Subjective: \n- Surgeries: gall bladder surgery, appendix surgery, and ovaries removal.\n- No severity, duration, associated symptoms, relevant medical history, family history, allergies, or specific concerns mentioned.\n\nObjective: \n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment: \n- No clinician's interpretation provided.\n\nPlan: \n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00008", "dialogue": "What brings you in? Yeah, I'm just coming in with my son, he's been having, he's been complaining of just, seems like pain in his right ear for the last I would say three to four days. And it seems like he's just more irritable, just not himself right now. Yeah OK, so, how long did you say he's had this ear pain? I would say four days now. Four days, OK. Have you noticed any hearing loss? Uh, no, he still responds when I call his name. OK and, have you noticed any fluid coming out of his ears? Uh, no, I don't think so. Is it more like one side of his ears or is it both ears? It's just his right ear. Just his right ear, OK. And have you noticed that it's like, swollen, red or warm? Uh, no, I don't see any swelling or redness at all. OK, OK, good, and have you taken a look inside his ears? Yeah, I tried to take a look with a flashlight. I couldn't really see too much. OK, yeah, that's fair. And you mentioned he's had a runny nose too, When did that start? Yeah, so that, kind of like last week he was recovering from, I think it was just a cold and he had a runny nose and just was a bit congested. But then over the last week it kind of just resolved. But then this week he started having just this ear pain. OK. Any fevers? Uh, no, not that I, I haven't measured anything and he hasn't mentioned any, like he hasn't felt cold or anything like that. OK, any weight loss over the past month or so? No. No, any change in his hair or nails, like rashes or, sorry, or skin? Yeah, no, I haven't seen any changes in the skin. OK, any changes in his vision or smell or taste? No. No, OK. His runny nose, what color is it? It was mainly, uh, it seemed to me like it was mainly clear when it was happening, like he wasn't bringing up any yellow or green material. OK, good. Does he have a sore throat at all? No, not that he's told me. He's still eating things and I don't think he's having any trouble with eating anything, so I don't think so. OK, that's good. Has he had a cough? Uhm, actually he did. He did start coughing like yesterday, but just a few times a day. I don't know if it's because of this or like, what else might be going on. OK, and you said for the past few days, he's had the cough? Yeah, I think just maybe started yesterday. Started yesterday, OK. Is it a dry cough or is it, is he bringing up any phlegm? No, it seems to be just dry. I think I just noticed it like three or four times during the day yesterday. OK, so no blood that he's bringing up right? No, no. OK, and does he have any shortness of breath? No, no. No, OK, any wheezing? No, no wheezing. No chest pain? No. OK, uhm, any nausea or vomiting? No nausea or vomiting. Any changes to his bowel movements? No. No, OK and, how about urine, is that normal? Yeah, Uh, no changes at all. Any headaches? Not that he's told me about. OK, dizziness or fainting spells? No. Feeling like he's off balance or lost coordination? No, I don't think so. No, OK, no numbness, no tingling? No. Has he had any contacts with sick people recently? Uhm, no, I don't think anyone's been sick around him. OK and has he traveled to anywhere recently? Uh, no, like, he's mainly just been inside his home. OK. has he ever had ear pain before? He did have a similar pain, I think when he was like around three years old, but it happened with his left left ear, not the right one. OK, was he prescribed any medications? Uh yeah, he was given a, like a course of antibiotics at that time. I forgot what it was called, but I think at that time it was much worse, like he, I don't know if he was just younger and couldn't tolerate it but I think he also had a fever at that time. OK, OK, and then any other ear infections outside of that one time. No, I think that, only that one I can recall. OK, alright, is he otherwise healthy? Yeah, otherwise he's healthy. No, other conditions or anything like that. No hospitalizations or surgeries? No. And is he on any medications? No medications. OK, is he up to date with all his immunizations? Yeah, all his immunizations are good. OK, any allergies? Not that we know of so far. And any family history of lung conditions or heart conditions. No lung or heart conditions. Just, his father does have like celiac disease, so I don't know if that's related at all. OK, how old was he when was diagnosed with celiac? Fairly young I would say, in his 20s. OK, OK, uhm, alright, we'll keep an eye on it in him, like if he has any changes in his bowel movements or if he's getting severe abdominal pain after eating certain foods with gluten you can get him checked up for that as well. OK. Yeah and then, in terms of where he lives, does he live with you and your partner? Yeah, so it's just, he's an only child, lives with me and my husband, and also we have like a pet dog. OK, do you live in an apartment or in a house? We live in a, it's like a condominium. OK, OK, any old carpet, mold or asbestos in the building? No, no, nothing like that. It's fairly well kept. OK, And is he other-wise like developmental-wise, he's OK? There's no past history of like developmental disorders, anything like that? No, no issues from like when he was younger like no complaints at all. I Think he was doing the same as every other child. OK, no complications during your pregnancy with him, no complications during the actual delivery and then when he was a newborn? Uh no, no complications, it was just a vaginal birth at 39 weeks and we didn't need to stay in the hospital at all afterwards. OK, good. So those were all the questions that I had for you today. Did you have any questions of your own? Yeah, I don't know if, uh, like last time we did get some antibiotics. I don't know if he needs them right now or what would you recommend? Yeah, uhm so I'm gonna have to take a look into his ears first before I can come up with like a management plan for him. So once I do, if I see, uhm, if his tympanic membrane has been perforated or I see pus in there, we'll prescribe him like 10 days of antibiotics. We can give him either Amoxicillin, or if he has bad reactions to penicillins, we can give him Cefuroxime and then if, uh, it doesn't seem to have any systemic symptoms, so if he has systemic symptoms like fever, which we'll check for today, and if he's like been really ill and he has like a middle ear effusion or bulging tympanic membrane, again we'll give him antibiotics for 10 days. But if his tympanic membrane is just bulging a little bit, or if he has some middle ear effusion but no systemic symptoms, like, no fevers, and he seems to be fine otherwise, we'll reassess him in 24 to 48 hours to see if he's better and if he's not, again, will prescribe him antibiotics. How does that sound? Yeah, that sounds good. Thank you. OK yeah, no worries.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 4 yo M presents with right ear pain x 4 days\n- No hearing loss, fluid from ears, swelling, redness or warmth of ear\n- Hx of recent URTI with runny nose (clear) and congestion, now resolved\n- New onset dry cough x 1 day (3-4 episodes/day, no hemoptysis)\n- Denies fever, weight loss, skin/hair/nail changes, vision/smell/taste changes, sore throat, SOB, wheezing, chest pain, N/V, change in bowel movements or urination, HA, dizziness, syncope, imbalance, numbness/tingling\n- Hx of similar left ear pain at age 3, treated with abx\n- No sick contacts or recent travel\n- Lives in well-kept condo with parents and pet dog, no known environmental exposures\n\nPMHx:\n- Prior left AOM at age 3\n- Otherwise healthy, no hospitalizations or surgeries\n- Father with celiac disease dx in 20s\n\nMedications: None\n\nAllergies: NKDA\n\nImmunizations: Up to date\n\nBirth Hx: FT SVD at 39 weeks, uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals pending\n- Ear exam pending\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely AOM\n\nPlan:\n- Assess tympanic membranes\n- If TM perforation or purulent fluid, treat with 10 day course of amoxicillin or cefuroxime (if PCN allergy)\n- If TM bulging or MEE with systemic symptoms (e.g. fever), treat with 10 day course of abx\n- If TM bulging or MEE without systemic symptoms, reassess in 24-48h and treat with abx if not improved"}
{"ID": "soap_00081", "dialogue": "Are you still taking Micronase every morning? Yes, I take um two point five M G once a day. Great. Continue taking Micronase by mouth every day. As for your infection, I want you to start taking Bactrim twice daily. I'll be prescribing you thirty pills. It'll be Bactrim four hundred M G and eighty M G tablets.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is taking Micronase 2.5 mg once daily.\n- Patient has an infection that is being addressed.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinical impressions or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Continue taking Micronase by mouth every day.\n- Start taking Bactrim 400 mg and 80 mg tablets twice daily.\n- Prescribing a total of thirty pills of Bactrim."}
{"ID": "soap_00020", "dialogue": "What brings you in here today? I've had this pain in my back thats kind of like in my lower back and my buttocks and it's now radiating down to my right leg. I've had this for awhile now, but it's getting worse and I just want to come get it checked out. OK, and how long have you had this pain for? About a month. OK, and has gone worse at all like did you have the it radiating through your leg before or is that something that happened after? That's something that happened after that happens that that's been going on for the past week. OK, and is this pain constant or does it come and go? And it's pretty much always there, but certain things can make it worse. Like if I'm walking or doing certain activities it can it can make it worse. OK. And did you have any back pain before this month? No, not, I mean no, not really. OK. An what kind of pain would you say it is. Is it like a sharp stabbing pain or is it more so a dull aching pain? Um usually or well before it was like a mild achy pain, but now it can be. It's sharp and it feels like a burning sensation. OK, a burning sensation, where would you say that burning sensation is? It's like down my buttocks to the right leg. OK, and do you feel any kind of numbness or tingling with it? Um a little, a little bit of tingling. Yeah, a little bit of tingling. OK, and how would you rate the pain from a scale of zero to 10, 10 being the worst pain you've had in your life? So usually it's about like a 5 to 6 out of 10, but when it gets bad and um sharp, I'd give it an 8 out of 10. OK, and are there any kind of motions or positions that cause you more pain? Yeah, so I'd say like just any sort of physical activity, like if I'm on my feet a lot, it hurts. Mmm. Yeah, that's pretty much the only thing that I can think of right now. OK, did anything happen one month ago or before this pain started. Like any injuries or falls that might have caused this pain? Um no like a long time ago, like 6-7 years ago I injured my back at work but. I just did some conservative treatments for that and it went away. OK was it your lower back. Yes. OK. And what do you do for work? I used to work on an assembly line, so I used to. Like carry heavy objects and things like that. Like car parts. OK, and do you still work there? No, I don't. OK when did you stop working there? About three years back. OK and is there any other work you do currently? Um I work from home now. I do daycare at home. OK. OK, and just getting back to the pain so it's kind of in your lower back, your buttocks and then it goes to your right upper leg? Mmmm Does it move anywhere else besides that or those are the regions that it stays in? No, those are kind of the only regions that I'm experiencing it right now. OK. And do you get more pain leaning bending forward or would you say extending your back? I would say ext.. Extending my back hurts more. OK and you get pain with sitting down? Um sometimes. OK, how about lying down? Um lying down I guess it depends on the position. If I am straight on my back it's it's not too bad, but if I like I'm hunched over or something like that, it hurts. OK, I see OK and have you tried any medications over the counter and painkillers that have helped? I tried some ibuprofen and some like topical.. um like ointments but it helps temporarily and then the pain comes back. OK, let's see and. Other than that, so other than this pain that's been going on do you have any fevers or chills? Uhm No, no fevers or chills. OK, no night sweats? No. Any increased fatigue? No, no. Any rashes or skin changes? No Any uh. Any cough or cold symptoms recently? Um no nothing like that. OK and apart from your back, any kind of joint or muscle pain anywhere else in your body? No. Any changes in your bowel movements? No. OK, any urinary problems? Nope. Any stool incontinence for your urinary incontinence? No. OK, and any kind of numbness or paresthesia like around just your lower back and buttocks? No, no numbness just like that, tingling, burning feeling. OK, OK any recent weight changes like any significant weight loss? No. OK. Any muscle weakness at all in that area? I'm finding it hard to walk. Harder to walk? OK. Are you limping at all? Yeah, I'm putting more weight on my left leg. OK. And do you feel, so you mention you feel tingling going down your right leg? Do you feel any kind of numbness at all? No. No, OK alright do you have any kind of urinary or fecal retention like at all? No, I'm going. I'm using the washroom OK. OK, uh. Do you have any history of any cancers at all? No. No OK. Are you on any. So do you have any past medical conditions you've been diagnosed with? No. No. Do you take any medications regularly? No, I don't. I just take calcium and vitamin D and other but multi vitamins. OK, any allergies to any medications? No. Have you had any previous surgeries or have you been hospitalized for any reason? No. No, OK, any family history of any autoimmune conditions, any heart or lung conditions? Um no not that I know of. OK, an and, currently you said you work from home. How would you describe your living situation like? I would say I it's it's OK. I live in an apartment with my husband. OK. And currently do you smoke? No. OK, and do you drink alcohol? I'll have a glass of wine like once a week. OK, and do you use any recreational drugs? Um so I've I've been using cannabis oil for the past month just to see if it helps with the pain. OK, and has that been working for you at all? A little bit I think. OK. OK, so those are some of the questions I had. And I'm just gonna ask a few questions about just examining the areas that are giving you trouble. So along your back, if you if you were to press down on from the middle to your lower back, any kind of pain when you press down. Um lower back, yes. OK, So what kind of pain is it like sharp or is it kind of a dull aching pain? When I press down it feels sharp. OK, and do you have you notice any kind of swelling in your legs or, near the areas where you have pain swelling or redness, or any warmth? Um it seems to. Actually, no, it's not. It's not warm. OK. And in terms of just movement, are you able to bend down? I can bend down, it's just hard for me to get back up. OK See and how about like bend sideways. I can do that. OK nd how about if you're laying flat are you able to raise your legs without bending your knees? I can do that, yeah, actually no. I can't. Sorry that that hurts. So your right leg hurts when you do that? Yeah OK, great. And other than that, like when you when you do any of those movements, can you, do you get like that tingling again? Yeah it does. Yeah. OK alright and when you stand up and walk how would you describe, are you having any kind of limping at all? Um a little bit of limp yeah, I'm putting more weight on the left leg. OK, alright, so yeah, those are just some of the questions I I had for you today. Do you have any questions at all? Um no, what can I do about this back pain and how long do you think I'll have it for? Yeah, so it's been happening for one month right and you're getting some symptoms in in your lower leg, so normally we would wait before we do any kind of diagnostic imaging. But since you're also having these other symptoms we we were worrying about sometimes lower back pain can lead to something called sciatica. It's just when one of the nerves get pinched, and then that's why you're getting the tingling around your right upper leg. So what we can do is. We can do if you haven't had an X Ray, we can do an X Ray of your lower back. And then we can also do an MRI. OK And. Based on what results come back at the same time we will, I will give you some information about some anti- inflammatory medications you could take to help relieve some of your pain, but other than that I will also refer you to some physiotherapy. That will help you do some exercises at home. Just ways to protect your back as well as strengthen some of your muscles to help deal with some of that pain. Yeah, so we'll get the results back. And then we'll go from there. OK, sounds good. Thank you. You're welcome. Take care.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 1-month history of lower back pain radiating to right buttock and leg\n- Pain is constant, described as mild aching initially, now sharp and burning (severity 5-6/10 usually, 8/10 at worst)\n- Burning sensation extends from right buttock to right leg\n- Mild tingling sensation in right leg\n- Pain worse with physical activity, walking, and standing for prolonged periods\n- Pain worse with back extension and certain lying positions\n- Ibuprofen and topical ointments provide temporary relief\n- Denies fevers, chills, night sweats, fatigue, rashes, cough, cold symptoms, joint/muscle pain elsewhere, bowel/bladder changes, urinary/fecal incontinence, weight changes\n- Reports difficulty walking and limping, favoring left leg\n- Using cannabis oil for past month for pain relief with mild improvement\n\nPMHx:\n- Lower back injury at work (assembly line) 6-7 years ago, resolved with conservative treatment\n- Denies other medical conditions\n- Medications: calcium, vitamin D, multivitamins\n- No known drug allergies\n- No previous surgeries or hospitalizations\n- No family history of autoimmune, heart, or lung conditions\n\nSocHx:\n- Lives in apartment with husband\n- Works from home running a daycare\n- Denies smoking\n- Alcohol use: 1 glass of wine per week\n- Cannabis oil use for past month for pain relief\n\nObjective:\n- Tenderness to palpation over lower back (sharp pain)\n- No swelling, redness, or warmth noted in painful areas\n- Able to bend forward and laterally\n- Unable to raise right leg while lying flat due to pain\n- Tingling sensation reproduced with movement\n- Mild limp noted, favoring left leg\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely sciatica secondary to nerve impingement\n\nPlan:\n- X-ray and MRI of lumbar spine\n- Prescribe anti-inflammatory medications for pain relief\n- Refer to physiotherapy for back protection techniques and strengthening exercises\n- Follow-up after imaging results"}
{"ID": "soap_00019", "dialogue": "Any history of heart attack? No. Any chest pain, palpitations or shortness of breath on exertion? No. How about your blood pressure? It's normal. Any swelling? No. Any history of irregular heartbeats? No.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No history of heart attack.\n- No chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath on exertion.\n- Patient reports normal blood pressure.\n- No swelling.\n- No history of irregular heartbeats.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00014", "dialogue": "Is there any history of heart disease in your family? Yes, my dad and brother had heart vessel blockage type of disease in their forties.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Family history of heart disease: father and brother had heart vessel blockage in their forties.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00006", "dialogue": "Are you allergic to anything? No, I am not allergic to anything. Okay so no drug allergies. Yeah.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No allergies reported (including drug allergies).\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinical impressions or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, or follow-up explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00011", "dialogue": "Have you had any surgeries in the past? No, none.", "note": "Subjective:\n- No past surgeries reported.\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00093", "dialogue": "Are you on any medications? No, not taking any medications.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is not taking any medications.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data mentioned.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No assessment provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No plan provided.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00065", "dialogue": "What brings you in here today? Hi I'm coming in because I've had this like on and off runny nose and stuffy nose for the past week. Um and I just came to make sure it's not something serious like Covid. OK, yeah no I completely understand why you might be worried um with everything going on. So just to get an idea of the timeline, so when did this start? So it started about I'd say, seven days ago. Seven days ago, OK. And was it just a runny nose that started or did you have any other symptoms? So at first I felt kind of stuffy um and then now it's more runny. Um and no other symptoms. And are you coughing at all? No. Are you bringing up any discharge from like your nose, or are you coughing any material up? No, no. I mean there's some discharge from my nose, like it's runny, but it's it's not yellow or anything, it's clear. OK, and do you think this issue has gone worse over the last seven days or is it improving at all? Um I don't think it's improving. It's changed 'cause it used to be stuffy, now it's more of a runny nose. Um but yeah, it seems to be runny, like it's the same amount of runniness for the past couple of days. And are you having any symptoms of loss of smell at all? No, no loss of smell. How about loss of taste? No. Um were you uh with anyone around you sick at all before those seven days? Uh I don't think so, I visited my sister and she has a nephew who goes to daycare, but my nephew's been OK, no, no runny nose or stuffy nose. Uhm, and is it just a constant issue that goes on throughout the day or is it worse at night, during the day, how would you describe it? Um it's it's pretty constant throughout the day. It's just it just feels bad at night because it um makes it hard for me to fall asleep. OK, uh have you tried any medications at all over the counter that would help? Um I tried some antihistamines because I do have seasonal allergies. Uh but they don't seem to be helping. Um any anything that makes your symptoms worse at all, like any exertion or any kind of uh position? No. Um do you have any shortness of breath or chest pain at all? Uh no. Any sore throat or muscle aches? No. Any uh headaches? Nope. Have you traveled at all recently? No. Have you had any nausea or vomiting? Uh no. Any fevers or chills? No. Any night sweats or fatigue? No. OK. Um any kind of sensation that your heart is racing? Uh no, nothing like that. Any changes in your bowel movements like any constipation or diarrhea? No. Any changes in your urinary pattern? No. Uh any issues with your sleep or appetite? No. Have you lost any weight, significant weight, uh recently in the last few months? No. And uh do you have any exposure to like fumes, dust, or like uh carpet, pets, or animals? Anything that you might have an allergic reaction to? Uh no nothing like that. OK and currently are you sexually active? I am. And um can you just tell me a little a little bit of a detail like about like a partner? Uh and if you've had any new partners recently at all? No, I've been with the same partner for the past five years. OK, and uh do you have any history of any STI's? No. OK nice, um, in terms of your uh past medical history, do you have any um diagnoses or anything any medical conditions at all? Uhm no, no nothing, nothing long standing. Um anything you take medications for? No. Are you allergic to any medications? Uh not medications. Uh any environmental allergies? Just yeah, some seasonal allergies. OK. Any previous hospitalizations or surgeries? Um I was hospitalized for um Like an appendectomy when I was younger. Um but that's about it. And when was that? That was when I was 18. And any complications after that? No. And uh in terms of your family history, does anyone have any lung conditions, heart conditions, or any cancers? Uh nope, nothing like that. And currently uh do you work? I do. I work from home though. OK. And how would you describe your living situation right now? Um I live in an apartment with um my partner and we have a dog. Um and currently, uh do you smoke at all? Uh no. Do you use any recreational drugs like marijuana? No. How about uh any alcohol? Uh I drink like a glass of wine every other day. OK. Um yeah, so those were some of the questions I had for you. I know it's a bit concerning because of the pandemic going on and um a runny nose can be a symptom uh of Covid as well, but uh it can also be a symptom of just the common cold or any other virus. Um but since you're having, uh you're symptomatic right now, we will definitely get like a Covid swab test done for you. OK. And if you start having any, just monitor for your symptoms. Um you should have the test back within 48 hours. Until then, uh at least for 14 days you should try to isolate in your own room, wear a mask around uh your partner, uh and not go outside, so just self isolate. OK. And and then you will get the test results back in a, hopefully in a few days. So do you have any questions at all? Uh no, thank you, that was good. Take care, bye You too.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Runny nose and stuffy nose for the past week\n- Initially stuffy, now more runny\n- Clear nasal discharge\n- Symptoms constant throughout the day, worse at night (difficulty falling asleep)\n- Tried antihistamines (ineffective)\n- No cough, loss of smell, loss of taste, SOB, chest pain, sore throat, muscle aches, headaches, nausea, vomiting, fever, chills, night sweats, fatigue, palpitations, changes in bowel movements, sleep or appetite issues, or significant weight loss\n- No recent travel\n- No exposure to fumes, dust, or animals\n\nPast Medical History:\n- PMHx: appendectomy at age 18 (no complications)\n- Seasonal allergies\n- No other medical conditions, medications, or allergies to medications\n- No previous hospitalisations or surgeries apart from appendectomy\n\nSocial History:\n- Lives in an apartment with partner and a dog\n- Works from home\n- No smoking, recreational drug use, or significant alcohol use (glass of wine every other day)\n- Sexually active with the same partner for the past 5 years\n\nFamily History:\n- No lung conditions, heart conditions, or cancers\n\nObjective:\n- No vitals or physical examination findings provided\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely diagnosis: Viral upper respiratory infection (common cold)\n- Differential diagnosis: COVID-19\n\nPlan:\n- Investigations: COVID-19 swab test\n- Treatment: Self-isolation for 14 days, wear a mask around partner, avoid going outside\n- Monitor symptoms, expect test results within 48 hours"}
{"ID": "soap_00087", "dialogue": "Do you live alone? I wouldn't say I live alone. I have three cats and they always keep me company. Aw, what kind of cats? Two Persian cats and one Chartreux. Are you currently working? I retired a few months ago. I used to work as a psych aide for the State of Pennsylvania. Congratulations on your retirement. Thank you. How long did you work as a psych aide? Thirty two years. Incredible. One more question. Do you smoke, drink, or use drugs recreationally? I drink wine every now and then. I used to smoke back in the day, but I'm five years smoke free. I do not do drugs. Always great to hear.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient has three cats (two Persian cats and one Chartreux).\n- Patient retired a few months ago after working for thirty-two years as a psych aide for the State of Pennsylvania.\n- Patient drinks wine occasionally.\n- Patient used to smoke but has been smoke-free for five years.\n- Patient does not use recreational drugs.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided."}
{"ID": "soap_00028", "dialogue": "Welcome back. Thank you. You are back for a follow up on your right arm pain, right? Yes. It has been almost a year since the pain first started. How has your arm been feeling? I still have this dull pain in my upper outer area of my arm. How long does the pain last? It was coming and going, but now the pain is more persistent since I last saw you. It is still happening every day. Remind me again. You are right handed. Right? Yeah. It makes is hard to do things with the persistent pain. I bet it does. Are you experiencing any numbness or tingling sensation in your arm or hand? No, but I have had this achy sensation in my hand radiating down to my fingers. Do you have a history of any other neck or shoulder injuries? It is not from a specific injury but I have had this neck pain for thirty years. Did you see anyone for the neck pain? Yes. I saw someone for it in two thousand and six, and then I developed this ear pain. They could not figure out what was going on so eventually I was sent to have an M R I of my neck and upper spine, which showed some degenerative changes. Then I was referred to Doctor X who was treating me for neck pain. He gave me steroid injections. Are you still seeing Doctor X for the epidural injections? Yes. I have been seeing him since two thousand and seven. How old are you? I am fifty eight. Are you experiencing any symptoms from receiving the injections? Those are for neck pain. My arm pain recent. I have been having the injections for a while now, without any trouble. What medications have you been taking for the pain? There are a few of them that I take. I gave the list to the nurse. Okay. I also have taken several Medrol packs. They make my blood sugars to increase so I try not to take it. Okay. Does the pain prevent you from doing your daily activities or decrease your quality of life? No. I can do everything like normal, but constant nagging pain does get to me at times.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Dull pain in the upper outer area of the right arm, persistent pain daily, achy sensation in hand radiating down to fingers.\n- Severity: Persistent pain that makes it hard to do things; described as constant nagging pain that gets to the patient at times.\n- Duration: Pain has been present for almost a year; has become more persistent since the last visit.\n- Associated symptoms: No numbness or tingling sensation in the arm or hand; history of neck pain for thirty years.\n- Relevant medical history: Previous neck pain treated in 2006; MRI showed degenerative changes; received steroid injections from Doctor X since 2007.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: Medrol packs increase blood sugars, so the patient tries not to take them; able to do daily activities normally despite the pain.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00046", "dialogue": "Hi, I am Doctor Chandler. Hello Doctor. So, tell me what's going on? I am having a headache.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Headache\n- Severity: Not specified\n- Duration: Not specified\n- Associated symptoms: Not specified\n- Relevant medical history: Not mentioned\n- Family history: Not mentioned\n- Allergies: Not mentioned\n- Other concerns: None explicitly mentioned\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00051", "dialogue": "Ready to go home? I just can't wait. Sure. I can understand. Bye Doctor. Yes. See you soon.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient expresses eagerness to go home.\n- Emotional state indicates excitement about discharge.\n\nObjective:\n- (No measurable or observable data mentioned.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned.)"}
